// The explorer's help: its manual, docs/src/explorer/*.md, as docs/explorer_help.jl
// writes it. Change the pages and run that script; this file is not edited by hand.
export const SITE = "https://ianlap.github.io/SigmaTau.jl/dev/";
export const TOPICS = [
  { id: "index", nav: "Overview", web: "https://ianlap.github.io/SigmaTau.jl/dev/explorer/", sections: [
    { id: "", level: 1, title: "The explorer", html: [
      "<h1>The explorer</h1>",
      "<p>The explorer is the package with a window. It opens a record, checks it and shows the deviations, the spectra, the dynamic deviation and the time error in holdover of one clock or of several, with every setting on screen. It also simulates clocks from a noise model, forms a time scale from a group of clocks and follows a measurement while it runs.</p>",
      "<p>Every result it shows is computed by the <a href=\"https://ianlap.github.io/SigmaTau.jl/dev/guide/cli/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><code>sigmatau</code></a> program, which runs the code of the Julia functions. The report of an analysis lists the command lines that repeat it.</p>",
      "<p>The explorer comes as a desktop app and as pages that a script serves to a browser. Both show the same pages, and <a href=\"#help/install\">Install and run</a> covers the two.</p>",
    ] },
    { id: "A-first-analysis", level: 2, title: "A first analysis", html: [
      "<h2>A first analysis</h2>",
      "<p>The explorer starts with a simulated record of 16384 phase samples at 1 s and its analysis, so that every page has something to show before a file is open.</p>",
      "<ol><li><b>Open the record.</b> On the <a href=\"#help/data\">Data</a> page press <b>Open a file…</b>, or drop a file anywhere in the window. The import dialog shows the top of the file and asks what each column holds, whether the readings are phase or fractional frequency, and in which unit.</li><li><b>Check it.</b> The record check lists the samples, the span, the gaps, the points past 5σ and the frequency drift, beside a plot of the phase. Drift removal, gap filling and outlier removal are set there.</li><li><b>Choose the estimators.</b> <b>+ Add estimator</b> in the <a href=\"#help/panel\">side panel</a> opens the list. The τ grid, the confidence level, the bias correction and the noise type are under <b>Options</b>.</li><li><b>Run the analysis</b> with <b>Run analysis</b> or F5.</li><li><b>Read the results</b> on the <a href=\"#help/stability\">Stability</a>, <a href=\"#help/spectrum\">Spectrum</a>, <a href=\"#help/dynamic\">Dynamic</a>, <a href=\"#help/holdover\">Holdover</a> and <a href=\"#help/steering\">Steering</a> pages.</li><li><b>Keep them.</b> The <b>File</b> menu saves the plot, the numbers, a report and the whole <a href=\"#help/export\">session</a>.</li></ol>",
    ] },
    { id: "The-window", level: 2, title: "The window", html: [
      "<h2>The window</h2>",
      "<table><thead><tr><th>Part</th><th>Holds</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Tabs</td><td>The nine pages: <a href=\"#help/data\">Data</a>, <a href=\"#help/synthesis\">Synthesis</a>, <a href=\"#help/stability\">Stability</a>, <a href=\"#help/spectrum\">Spectrum</a>, <a href=\"#help/dynamic\">Dynamic</a>, <a href=\"#help/holdover\">Holdover</a>, <a href=\"#help/steering\">Steering</a>, <a href=\"#help/ensemble\">Ensemble</a> and <a href=\"#help/live\">Live</a></td></tr><tr><td>Record chip</td><td>The record in use: its name, the number of samples, the sample interval, the span and what was removed from it. With several clocks, their number and the samples in all. A click opens the Data page. A window 1100 px wide or narrower leaves the chip out</td></tr><tr><td><b>File</b> menu</td><td>The <a href=\"#help/export\">exports</a>: the plot, the results, a report, and saving and opening a session</td></tr><tr><td>Theme button</td><td>Switches between the colors of the system, a light theme and a dark theme. The choice is remembered</td></tr><tr><td><b>?</b></td><td>The <a href=\"#help/index/The-help\">help</a>: a search through this manual and its sections on the page in view</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"#help/panel\">Side panel</a></td><td>The clocks, the estimators and the options of the analysis, with the <b>Run analysis</b> button. On Synthesis, Ensemble and Live it holds the settings of that page</td></tr><tr><td>Status bar</td><td>Whether the results on screen belong to the inputs on screen</td></tr></tbody></table>",
    ] },
    { id: "When-results-are-computed", level: 2, title: "When results are computed", html: [
      "<h2>When results are computed</h2>",
      "<p>A change to the record, to the estimators or to the options does not start an analysis, and neither does a file that has just been opened. The Run button and the status bar turn amber, and the Stability and Spectrum pages keep the results of the last run until <b>Run analysis</b> is pressed again. A run can be canceled, and the results from before then stay on screen.</p>",
      "<p>The explorer runs the analysis by itself in five cases: at start, on its simulated record; when the reference clock changes and there are results; when a session is opened; when the readings of a live measurement are loaded; and after each change to a noise model built by hand.</p>",
      "<table><thead><tr><th>Shown</th><th>Computed</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Stability, Spectrum</td><td>By <b>Run analysis</b></td></tr><tr><td>The record check and the phase plot of Data</td><td>When the record or its preparation changes</td></tr><tr><td>The frequency drift fit of Data</td><td>When its model changes</td></tr><tr><td>Dynamic, Holdover, Steering</td><td>When the page is shown and the record or its settings have changed</td></tr><tr><td>The predicted stability of Synthesis</td><td>When a value is entered</td></tr><tr><td>Ensemble</td><td>When the page is shown and the clocks or its settings have changed</td></tr><tr><td>Live</td><td>With every batch of readings</td></tr></tbody></table>",
    ] },
    { id: "Keyboard-and-mouse", level: 2, title: "Keyboard and mouse", html: [
      "<h2>Keyboard and mouse</h2>",
      "<table><thead><tr><th>Key</th><th>Action</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>F5, or Ctrl+Enter (Cmd+Enter on macOS)</td><td>Run the analysis. During a run: cancel it, or start it again if the inputs have changed since</td></tr><tr><td>1 to 9</td><td>Data, Synthesis, Stability, Spectrum, Dynamic, Holdover, Steering, Ensemble, Live</td></tr><tr><td>?</td><td>The help menu</td></tr><tr><td>F1</td><td>The help on the page in view</td></tr><tr><td>Esc</td><td>Cancel a run, close a dialog or the help menu</td></tr></tbody></table>",
      "<p>On a plot, hovering shows the values, a drag zooms and a double click restores the full range.</p>",
    ] },
    { id: "The-help", level: 2, title: "The help", html: [
      "<h2>The help</h2>",
      "<p>The help is this manual inside the explorer, without its screenshots. <b>?</b> in the top bar, or the ? key, opens the help menu:</p>",
      "<table><thead><tr><th>Entry</th><th>Opens</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><b>Search the help</b></td><td>The sections in which every word typed begins a word, those with the words in their title first. <code>tau</code>, <code>sigma</code> and the names of the other Greek letters of the manual also find the letter. Enter opens the first section listed</td></tr><tr><td><b>On this page</b></td><td>The sections of the manual on the page in view</td></tr><tr><td><b>Contents of the help</b></td><td>The manual from its first page, with its pages listed beside the text</td></tr><tr><td><b>Keyboard and mouse</b>, <b>Estimators and noise types</b></td><td>Those two parts of the manual</td></tr><tr><td><b>Documentation on the web</b></td><td>The documentation of the package in the browser, with the reference of the Julia functions and of the command line</td></tr></tbody></table>",
      "<p>F1 opens the help on the page in view. A link from the help to another part of the documentation opens in the browser.</p>",
    ] },
  ] },
  { id: "install", nav: "Install and run", web: "https://ianlap.github.io/SigmaTau.jl/dev/explorer/install/", sections: [
    { id: "", level: 1, title: "Install and run", html: [
      "<h1>Install and run</h1>",
      "<p>The explorer is a set of pages and the <a href=\"https://ianlap.github.io/SigmaTau.jl/dev/guide/cli/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><code>sigmatau</code></a> program, which does every computation. The desktop app holds both. From a checkout of the repository, a short script serves the pages to a browser and runs the program for them.</p>",
    ] },
    { id: "Desktop-app", level: 2, title: "Desktop app", html: [
      "<h2>Desktop app</h2>",
      "<p>A release carries the app for macOS, on Apple silicon and on Intel, and for Linux on x86-64:</p>",
      "<table><thead><tr><th>System</th><th>File</th><th>Install</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>macOS</td><td><code>SigmaTau-Explorer-&lt;version&gt;-macos-&lt;arch&gt;.dmg</code></td><td>Open it and drag the app to Applications</td></tr><tr><td>Linux</td><td>the <code>.deb</code> package</td><td><code>sudo apt install ./&lt;file&gt;.deb</code></td></tr><tr><td>Linux</td><td>the <code>.AppImage</code></td><td><code>chmod +x &lt;file&gt;.AppImage</code>, then run it</td></tr></tbody></table>",
      "<p>The app needs no Julia, no Python and no network. It takes over 100 MB on disk, most of it the Julia runtime of the program inside.</p>",
      "<p>macOS stops the first launch of a downloaded copy that Apple has not notarized. Right-click the app and choose <b>Open</b>, then <b>Open</b> again. On macOS 15, open it once and then allow it under System Settings, Privacy &amp; Security. Clearing the download flag does the same:</p>",
      "<pre><code>xattr -dr com.apple.quarantine &quot;/Applications/SigmaTau Explorer.app&quot;</code></pre>",
      "<p>The AppImage needs FUSE 2 (<code>sudo dnf install fuse</code> on Fedora, <code>sudo apt install libfuse2</code> on Ubuntu), or the option <code>--appimage-extract-and-run</code>.</p>",
    ] },
    { id: "In-a-browser,-from-the-repository", level: 2, title: "In a browser, from the repository", html: [
      "<h2>In a browser, from the repository</h2>",
      "<p>With Python 3 and a checkout of the repository:</p>",
      "<pre><code>python3 app/web/serve.py --open</code></pre>",
      "<p>The script serves the pages at <code>http://127.0.0.1:9385</code> and opens them in the default browser. It uses the Python standard library alone.</p>",
      "<table><thead><tr><th>Option</th><th>Effect</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>--open</code></td><td>Open the pages in the default browser</td></tr><tr><td><code>--port N</code></td><td>Serve on port <code>N</code> in place of 9385</td></tr><tr><td><code>--host ADDRESS</code></td><td>Listen on another address in place of <code>127.0.0.1</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>--fetch-plotly</code></td><td>Save the Plotly library to <code>app/web/ui/vendor</code> and exit</td></tr></tbody></table>",
      "<p>The script looks for the program in this order:</p>",
      "<ol><li>the path in the environment variable <code>SIGMATAU_CLI</code>;</li><li><code>cli/build/bin/sigmatau</code>, where the <a href=\"https://ianlap.github.io/SigmaTau.jl/dev/guide/cli/#Install\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">build</a> leaves it;</li><li><code>julia --project=cli -m SigmaTauCLI</code>, which needs Julia 1.13 or later and the packages of <code>cli</code> installed (<code>julia --project=cli -e 'using Pkg; Pkg.instantiate()'</code>).</li></ol>",
      "<p>The third way needs no build, but every request then waits for Julia to start and to compile the program, about half a minute each time. With the compiled program the first analysis of the explorer, four deviations with the spectra and the noise model of 16384 samples, returns in a fraction of a second.</p>",
      "<aside class=\"warning\"><h4>The script has no access control</h4><p>Whoever reaches its address can run the program on this computer, on a record sent with the request or on a simulated one, and can start a measurement. Keep the default address, <code>127.0.0.1</code>, which only this computer reaches, unless everyone on the network is trusted. On another address the <a href=\"#help/live\">Live</a> page reads from the simulator and from the network only, and not from a serial port or a file, so that no request reads a file of this computer.</p></aside>",
      "<p>The pages draw with <a href=\"https://plotly.com/javascript/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Plotly</a>, which they load from a content delivery network unless <code>--fetch-plotly</code> has saved a copy. Without a network and without that copy there are no plots.</p>",
    ] },
    { id: "Building-the-desktop-app", level: 2, title: "Building the desktop app", html: [
      "<h2>Building the desktop app</h2>",
      "<p>With the program built in <code>cli/build</code>, Rust and the Tauri command line (<code>cargo install tauri-cli --version &quot;^2&quot;</code>):</p>",
      "<pre><code>python3 app/web/serve.py --fetch-plotly    # optional: plots without a network\napp/desktop/bundle.sh                      # writes app/desktop/dist/</code></pre>",
      "<p>On macOS the script writes the app with a <code>.dmg</code> and a <code>.zip</code> of it, and on Linux a <code>.deb</code> and an <code>.AppImage</code>. The Linux build needs the WebKitGTK development packages. <code>app/desktop/README.md</code> in the repository lists them and covers signing and notarization on macOS.</p>",
      "<p><code>cd app/desktop &amp;&amp; cargo tauri dev</code> runs the app from the source tree without bundling it.</p>",
    ] },
  ] },
  { id: "panel", nav: "The side panel", web: "https://ianlap.github.io/SigmaTau.jl/dev/explorer/panel/", sections: [
    { id: "", level: 1, title: "The side panel", html: [
      "<h1>The side panel</h1>",
      "<p>The side panel holds what an analysis is made of: the clocks, the estimators and the options, with the <b>Run analysis</b> button below them. The Data, Stability, Spectrum, Dynamic, Holdover and Steering pages share it. The Synthesis, Ensemble and Live pages show their own settings in its place.</p>",
      "<p>With one clock, the Spectrum, Dynamic, Holdover and Steering pages put their settings in the panel too, in place of the estimators and options. With several clocks the panel keeps its content, and those four pages show their settings in a bar above the plots.</p>",
    ] },
    { id: "Clocks", level: 2, title: "Clocks", html: [
      "<h2>Clocks</h2>",
      "<p>The list of clocks appears when more than one is loaded. Clocks are added by <b>+ Add clock</b>, which opens a file, by marking several columns of one file as clocks in the <a href=\"#help/data/The-import-dialog\">import dialog</a>, by simulating several on the <a href=\"#help/data/Simulated-records\">Data</a> or <a href=\"#help/synthesis\">Synthesis</a> page, and by loading the readings of a <a href=\"#help/live\">live measurement</a>.</p>",
      "<p>Each row shows the color of the clock on every plot, its name, where it comes from and the number of its samples. There are eight colors, used again from the ninth clock on.</p>",
      "<table><thead><tr><th>Control</th><th>Action</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>A click on the row</td><td>Shows that clock on the Data page</td></tr><tr><td>◉</td><td>Hides the clock: it leaves the plots, the tables and the report. A run still analyzes it, and the TSV and JSON exports hold its results. ○ shows it again</td></tr><tr><td>×</td><td>Removes the clock. Removing one of several clocks that were simulated together simulates the others again</td></tr></tbody></table>",
    ] },
    { id: "Reference", level: 3, title: "Reference", html: [
      "<h3>Reference</h3>",
      "<p>Clocks read from one file were measured against one reference at the same instants. <b>Reference</b> exchanges that reference for one of the clocks: the record of every other clock of the file becomes its readings less those of the reference clock, <span class=\"math\"><i>x</i><sub><i>i</i></sub> − <i>x</i><sub><i>R</i></sub></span>, and the reference clock leaves the plots. This shows clock B against clock A when both were measured against a third. <b>As measured</b> returns to the records as read.</p>",
      "<p>Clocks from other files share neither the reference nor the instants. They cannot be chosen as the reference, and they stay as measured when one is chosen.</p>",
    ] },
    { id: "Estimators", level: 2, title: "Estimators", html: [
      "<h2>Estimators</h2>",
      "<p>The list holds the estimators that <b>Run analysis</b> computes. With one clock each estimator has a color. With several clocks the color is that of the clock, and each estimator has a line style. × removes an estimator.</p>",
      "<p><b>+ Add estimator</b> opens the list of all sixteen, with a field that searches their names and descriptions:</p>",
      "<table><thead><tr><th>Group</th><th>Estimators</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Allan family</td><td>ADEV, MDEV, HDEV, MHDEV, PDEV</td></tr><tr><td>Total</td><td>TOTDEV, MTOTDEV, HTOTDEV, MHTOTDEV</td></tr><tr><td>Thêo</td><td>THEO1, THEOH</td></tr><tr><td>Time</td><td>TDEV, HTDEV, TTOTDEV, MTIE, TIE rms</td></tr></tbody></table>",
      "<p>The first three groups are fractional frequency deviations <span class=\"math\">σ<sub><i>y</i></sub>(τ)</span> and the last is time deviations <span class=\"math\">σ<sub><i>x</i></sub>(τ)</span>, in seconds. The Stability page shows one kind at a time. <a href=\"#help/estimators\">Estimators and noise types</a> says what each one gives, which of them need a record without gaps and which take seconds on a long record.</p>",
    ] },
    { id: "Options", level: 2, title: "Options", html: [
      "<h2>Options</h2>",
      "<p>The options apply to every clock and every estimator. With several clocks they are folded under the heading <b>Options</b>, which shows the grid and the confidence level.</p>",
      "<table><thead><tr><th>Option</th><th>Choices</th><th>Effect</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><b>τ grid</b></td><td>Octave, Half octave, Quarter octave, Decade, Half decade, All τ</td><td>The averaging times: 1, 2, 4, … sample intervals for the octave grid, and every one that the record allows for All τ</td></tr><tr><td><b>Confidence intervals</b></td><td>On or off, at 68.3 %, 90 %, 95 % or 99 %</td><td>The level of the <a href=\"https://ianlap.github.io/SigmaTau.jl/dev/explanation/confidence/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">confidence intervals</a>. Off gives the deviations alone, without intervals, EDF or noise types</td></tr><tr><td><b>τ range (s)</b></td><td>A smallest and a largest averaging time</td><td>Leaves out the averaging times outside the limits. An empty field is no limit</td></tr><tr><td><b>Bias correction</b></td><td>Exact, Published tables, As Stable32, None</td><td>The <a href=\"https://ianlap.github.io/SigmaTau.jl/dev/explanation/bias/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">bias correction</a> of TOTDEV, MTOTDEV, HTOTDEV, MHTOTDEV, TTOTDEV and Thêo1</td></tr><tr><td><b>Noise type</b></td><td>Identified at each τ, From the fitted model, or one of the seven noise types</td><td>The noise type behind the intervals and the bias factors</td></tr></tbody></table>",
      "<p>The explorer starts with the octave grid, intervals at 68.3 %, the exact bias correction and the noise type identified at each τ.</p>",
      "<p>The four bias corrections are those of the keyword <code>correct_bias</code> of the estimators: <b>Exact</b> is <code>true</code>, <b>Published tables</b> is <code>:published</code>, <b>As Stable32</b> is <code>:stable32</code> and <b>None</b> is <code>false</code>.</p>",
      "<p><b>Identified at each τ</b> takes the noise type from <a href=\"https://ianlap.github.io/SigmaTau.jl/dev/explanation/noise_identification/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">noise identification</a>. <b>From the fitted model</b> takes the strongest term, at each averaging time, of the power-law model fitted to the record, the one that <b>Fit</b> shows on the <a href=\"#help/stability/The-noise-model\">Stability</a> page. A stated type holds for every averaging time, for a record whose noise is known.</p>",
    ] },
    { id: "Running", level: 2, title: "Running", html: [
      "<h2>Running</h2>",
      "<p><b>Run analysis</b> computes every estimator in the list for every clock but the reference, together with the spectra, the noise model and the mask checks. F5 and Ctrl+Enter do the same. The button turns amber when the record, the estimators or the options have changed since the last run, and it reads <b>Cancel</b> during a run. The line below it gives the number of estimators, the number of samples or clocks and the time the last run took.</p>",
    ] },
  ] },
  { id: "data", nav: "Data", web: "https://ianlap.github.io/SigmaTau.jl/dev/explorer/data/", sections: [
    { id: "", level: 1, title: "Data", html: [
      "<h1>Data</h1>",
      "<p>The Data page holds the record: where it comes from, how it was read, what is in it and what is done to it before every analysis. With several clocks it shows one of them, the one clicked in the <a href=\"#help/panel/Clocks\">list of clocks</a>.</p>",
    ] },
    { id: "Opening-a-file", level: 2, title: "Opening a file", html: [
      "<h2>Opening a file</h2>",
      "<p><b>Open a file…</b> opens a text file of readings, and a file dropped anywhere in the window opens the same way. Once a file is loaded the card shows its name and how it was read, with three buttons:</p>",
      "<table><thead><tr><th>Button</th><th>Action</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><b>Import settings…</b></td><td>Opens the import dialog again for this file</td></tr><tr><td><b>Open another file…</b></td><td>Puts another file in the place of this clock</td></tr><tr><td><b>+ Add clock</b></td><td>Opens a file as a further clock and keeps those loaded</td></tr></tbody></table>",
      "<p>The explorer reads the files that <a href=\"https://ianlap.github.io/SigmaTau.jl/dev/reference/data/#SigmaTau.read_data\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><code>read_data</code></a> reads:</p>",
      "<ul><li>text in columns, separated by blanks, commas or tabs, with <code>NaN</code> for a missing sample. Lines that start with <code>#</code> are skipped, and a first line of names is taken as the names of the columns;</li><li>Stable32 phase and frequency files (<code>.phd</code>, <code>.frd</code>) and TimeLab files (<code>.tim</code>), with the sample interval from their headers;</li><li>the readings of a counter, one per line in the number format of SCPI (<code>+9.999999999E+06</code>): frequencies in hertz become fractional frequency, and time intervals become phase;</li><li>tables with named columns and ISO 8601 timestamps;</li><li>the BIPM files of UTC − UTC(k);</li><li>RINEX clock files (<code>.clk</code>), from which the clocks are chosen in a list.</li></ul>",
      "<p>A file compressed with gzip (<code>.gz</code>) is unpacked as it is opened.</p>",
    ] },
    { id: "The-import-dialog", level: 2, title: "The import dialog", html: [
      "<h2>The import dialog</h2>",
      "<p>The dialog shows the top of the file and what the explorer proposes to read from it. The proposal comes from the names of the columns, the header and the size of the values. Check it before loading, the record type above all.</p>",
      "<p>The heading of each column is a menu:</p>",
      "<table><thead><tr><th>Choice</th><th>The column holds</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><b>Ignore</b></td><td>Nothing to read</td></tr><tr><td><b>Time (s)</b>, <b>Time (days, MJD)</b>, <b>Time (h)</b>, <b>Time (min)</b></td><td>The time of each reading, in that unit</td></tr><tr><td><b>Clock</b></td><td>The readings of one clock</td></tr></tbody></table>",
      "<p>Every column marked <b>Clock</b> becomes a clock, all measured against the same reference at the same instants. A click on the name of a column adds it as a clock or drops it.</p>",
      "<table><thead><tr><th>Setting</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><b>Record type</b></td><td><b>Phase (time error)</b> or <b>Fractional frequency</b></td></tr><tr><td><b>Phase units</b>, <b>Frequency units</b></td><td>Seconds to picoseconds for phase, and fractional, ppm, ppb or ppt for frequency. The field below takes a factor instead, written as an expression of numbers, <code>pi</code>, <code>+ - * /</code> and parentheses, such as <code>1/(2*pi*5e14)</code> for the phase of an optical carrier in radians</td></tr><tr><td><b>Sample interval τ₀</b></td><td>The step of the time column, or the interval that the header states. Without either it has to be typed</td></tr><tr><td><b>Measured against</b></td><td>The name of the reference, shown beside <b>As measured</b> in the list of clocks</td></tr><tr><td><b>Clock name</b>, <b>Clock names</b></td><td>The name of each clock on the plots and in the exports</td></tr><tr><td><b>Remember for files like this</b></td><td>Reads the next file with the same extension, number of columns and column names without the dialog</td></tr></tbody></table>",
      "<p>When the time column skips readings, the dialog counts the gaps and the missing samples. They enter the record as missing samples.</p>",
      "<p>In a Stable32 file (<code>.phd</code>, <code>.frd</code>) a zero marks a missing sample, except the first and the last sample of a phase file. The dialog counts these zeros and reads them as missing samples. <b>Keep zeros as readings</b> reads them as samples, for a record whose zeros are readings, as quantized data can have.</p>",
      "<p>A file that is read with remembered settings says so in the status bar. A Stable32 file with zeros opens the dialog all the same, unless the remembered settings keep them. <b>Import settings…</b> on the Data page opens the dialog for it. Files of different kinds that share an extension and a layout share the remembered settings too, so check the line under the file name.</p>",
    ] },
    { id: "Simulated-records", level: 2, title: "Simulated records", html: [
      "<h2>Simulated records</h2>",
      "<p><b>Synthesize</b> makes a record in place of a file, from the number of samples, the sample interval and a seed. Its noise is white FM with a random walk of the frequency, of <span class=\"math\">10<sup>−12</sup></span> and <span class=\"math\">10<sup>−14</sup></span> per sample as standard deviations. The same seed gives the same record.</p>",
      "<table><thead><tr><th>Field</th><th>Range</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><b>Clocks</b></td><td>1 to 8</td></tr><tr><td><b>Samples</b></td><td>64 to 10,000,000</td></tr><tr><td><b>τ₀ (s)</b></td><td>The sample interval</td></tr><tr><td><b>Seed</b></td><td>A whole number</td></tr></tbody></table>",
      "<p>With several clocks, each is noisier than the one before by a factor of two in deviation, and all are simulated against one ideal reference. The <a href=\"#help/ensemble\">Ensemble</a> page forms its time scale from them. The <a href=\"#help/synthesis\">Synthesis</a> page simulates clocks from a noise model of your own.</p>",
    ] },
    { id: "The-record-check", level: 2, title: "The record check", html: [
      "<h2>The record check</h2>",
      "<p>The record check is computed when the record is read and again whenever its preparation changes. It describes the record as prepared.</p>",
      "<table><thead><tr><th>Line</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><b>Samples</b></td><td>The number of phase samples, with the missing ones. A frequency record of <span class=\"math\"><i>N</i></span> readings has <span class=\"math\"><i>N</i> + 1</span></td></tr><tr><td><b>Span</b></td><td>The number of samples times the sample interval</td></tr><tr><td><b>Start</b></td><td>The time of the first sample in UTC, for a file with timestamps</td></tr><tr><td><b>Gaps</b></td><td>The number of missing samples</td></tr><tr><td><b>Points past 5σ</b></td><td>The outliers that <a href=\"https://ianlap.github.io/SigmaTau.jl/dev/reference/preprocessing/#SigmaTau.find_outliers\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><code>find_outliers</code></a> finds: frequency samples more than five scaled median absolute deviations from the median. They are marked on the phase plot</td></tr><tr><td><b>Frequency drift</b></td><td>The slope of a least-squares line through the fractional frequency, per day. It reads <b>Drift left</b> after a quadratic removal</td></tr></tbody></table>",
    ] },
    { id: "Preparing-the-record", level: 2, title: "Preparing the record", html: [
      "<h2>Preparing the record</h2>",
      "<p>Three steps are applied before every analysis, the check and the plots included. <a href=\"https://ianlap.github.io/SigmaTau.jl/dev/guide/preparing/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Gaps, outliers and drift</a> says when each is called for.</p>",
      "<p><b>Drift removal</b> fits the phase by least squares over the samples that are not missing and removes the fit. <b>Linear</b> removes a line, which takes out a frequency offset. <b>Quadratic</b> removes a parabola, which takes out a linear frequency drift as well. The check then lists the offset and the drift that were removed.</p>",
      "<p><b>Fill gaps by reflection</b> fills the missing samples as <a href=\"https://ianlap.github.io/SigmaTau.jl/dev/reference/preprocessing/#SigmaTau.fillgaps\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><code>fillgaps</code></a> does, from the samples beside each gap, reflected into it. The missing samples are the readings that the time column skips, those written as <code>NaN</code> and the zeros of a Stable32 file that are read as missing. For a frequency record that has any of them it is on from the start, since a frequency record cannot be read with samples missing.</p>",
      "<p><b>remove</b>, beside the count of outliers, replaces them as <a href=\"https://ianlap.github.io/SigmaTau.jl/dev/reference/preprocessing/#SigmaTau.remove_outliers\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><code>remove_outliers</code></a> does. The missing samples of the record stay missing. <b>undo</b> brings the outliers back.</p>",
      "<aside class=\"note\"><h4>Records with gaps</h4><p>A phase record with missing samples is analyzed as it is by the estimators that leave them out (<a href=\"#help/estimators\">Estimators and noise types</a>). An estimator that needs a complete record stops the run with an error, and the <a href=\"#help/spectrum/Records-with-gaps\">Spectrum</a> page has no spectrum for the record. A frequency record with missing samples cannot be read while <b>Fill gaps by reflection</b> is off, and the record check then keeps that box alone, to turn the filling back on.</p></aside>",
    ] },
    { id: "The-phase-plot-and-the-analysis-range", level: 2, title: "The phase plot and the analysis range", html: [
      "<h2>The phase plot and the analysis range</h2>",
      "<p>The <b>Phase</b> view plots the phase of the record as prepared, with the outliers marked. A drag zooms, and a double click restores the full range. The curve breaks where samples are missing. A record of more than 4000 samples is cut into 1332 stretches, and the curve runs through the lowest and the highest sample of each and through the two ends of the record, so that it keeps every outlying sample.</p>",
      "<p><b>Select range</b> turns the drag into a choice of the part of the record to analyze. The range is shaded, and the line above the plot gives its limits. It needs at least 16 samples. The Stability, Spectrum, Dynamic, Holdover and Steering pages then use that part alone, with the drift removal fitted to it. The check and the plot keep the whole record. <b>whole record</b> clears the range. Each clock has its own.</p>",
    ] },
    { id: "Frequency-drift", level: 2, title: "Frequency drift", html: [
      "<h2>Frequency drift</h2>",
      "<p>The <b>Frequency drift</b> view plots the fractional frequency of the record as prepared and fits one of four models to it by least squares, as <a href=\"https://ianlap.github.io/SigmaTau.jl/dev/reference/preprocessing/#SigmaTau.drift_fit\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><code>drift_fit</code></a> does:</p>",
      "<table><thead><tr><th>Model</th><th>Frequency</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><b>Linear</b></td><td><span class=\"math\"><i>a</i> + <i>b</i> <i>t</i></span></td></tr><tr><td><b>Quadratic</b></td><td><span class=\"math\"><i>a</i> + <i>b</i> <i>t</i> + <i>c</i> <i>t</i><sup>2</sup></span></td></tr><tr><td><b>Log</b></td><td><span class=\"math\"><i>a</i> ln(1 + <i>b</i> <i>t</i>) + <i>c</i></span></td></tr><tr><td><b>Diffusion</b></td><td><span class=\"math\"><i>a</i> + <i>b</i>√<span class=\"rad\"><i>t</i> + <i>c</i></span></span></td></tr></tbody></table>",
      "<p>The line above the plot gives the drift rate at the end of the record, per day, with its standard error and the rms of the residuals. Hovering over it shows the coefficients. <b>Residuals</b> plots what the model leaves.</p>",
      "<p>The standard error assumes white residuals. It is too small where they are not white, as on a record with flicker or random-walk FM.</p>",
      "<p>To fit the drift of the record as measured, set <b>Drift removal</b> to <b>None</b>: the view shows the frequency after the removal.</p>",
    ] },
  ] },
  { id: "synthesis", nav: "Synthesis", web: "https://ianlap.github.io/SigmaTau.jl/dev/explorer/synthesis/", sections: [
    { id: "", level: 1, title: "Synthesis", html: [
      "<h1>Synthesis</h1>",
      "<p>The Synthesis page defines the noise of up to eight simulated clocks, plots the stability that each noise model predicts, and simulates the clocks. It answers what a clock of a given noise looks like in a deviation, and it makes records with known noise for trying estimators and time scales.</p>",
    ] },
    { id: "The-noise-of-a-clock", level: 2, title: "The noise of a clock", html: [
      "<h2>The noise of a clock</h2>",
      "<p>A clock is the sum of up to four noises. Each has two fields, which hold the same level in two forms: the coefficient of the <a href=\"https://ianlap.github.io/SigmaTau.jl/dev/explanation/clock_model/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">clock model</a> and the coefficient <span class=\"math\"><i>h</i><sub>α</sub></span> of the power law <span class=\"math\"><i>S</i><sub><i>y</i></sub>(<i>f</i>) = <i>h</i><sub>α</sub><i>f</i><sup>α</sup></span>. Typing one fills in the other. A level of 0 leaves the noise out.</p>",
      "<table><thead><tr><th>Noise</th><th>Model</th><th>Unit</th><th>Power law</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>White PM</td><td><span class=\"math\"><i>R</i></span></td><td>s²</td><td><span class=\"math\"><i>h</i><sub>2</sub> = 8π<sup>2</sup>τ<sub>0</sub><i>R</i></span></td></tr><tr><td>White FM</td><td><span class=\"math\"><i>q</i><sub>1</sub></span></td><td>s</td><td><span class=\"math\"><i>h</i><sub>0</sub> = 2<i>q</i><sub>1</sub></span></td></tr><tr><td>Random-walk FM</td><td><span class=\"math\"><i>q</i><sub>2</sub></span></td><td>1/s</td><td><span class=\"math\"><i>h</i><sub>−2</sub> = <i>q</i><sub>2</sub>/(2π<sup>2</sup>)</span></td></tr><tr><td>Random-run FM</td><td><span class=\"math\"><i>q</i><sub>3</sub></span></td><td>1/s³</td><td><span class=\"math\"><i>h</i><sub>−4</sub> = <i>q</i><sub>3</sub>/(8π<sup>4</sup>)</span></td></tr></tbody></table>",
      "<p>The conversions are those of <a href=\"https://ianlap.github.io/SigmaTau.jl/dev/reference/spectra/#SigmaTau.ClockModel\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><code>ClockModel</code></a>. <span class=\"math\"><i>R</i></span> is the variance of the white PM of one phase sample, so the <span class=\"math\"><i>h</i><sub>2</sub></span> that goes with it changes with the sample interval. The page offers no flicker noise. <a href=\"https://ianlap.github.io/SigmaTau.jl/dev/reference/statistics/#SigmaTau.noise_gen\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><code>noise_gen</code></a> simulates it in Julia.</p>",
      "<p><b>Add a clock</b> adds a clock with the noise of the last one, and <b>Remove</b> takes one out. The name of a clock is the field beside its color.</p>",
    ] },
    { id: "Predicted-stability", level: 2, title: "Predicted stability", html: [
      "<h2>Predicted stability</h2>",
      "<p>The plot shows the deviation that each model predicts, at the averaging times of the octave grid for a record of <b>Samples</b> samples at the sample interval <b>τ₀</b>. <b>Preview</b> chooses MDEV, ADEV or MHDEV. The curves are computed in closed form, as <a href=\"https://ianlap.github.io/SigmaTau.jl/dev/reference/spectra/#SigmaTau.powerlaw_sigma\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><code>powerlaw_sigma</code></a> does, and they follow each value as it is entered. Nothing is simulated for them.</p>",
      "<p>ADEV and MDEV do not converge for random-run FM. A clock that has it is drawn in MHDEV alone.</p>",
    ] },
    { id: "Generating-the-clocks", level: 2, title: "Generating the clocks", html: [
      "<h2>Generating the clocks</h2>",
      "<p><b>Generate</b> simulates the clocks with <b>Samples</b>, <b>τ₀</b> and <b>Seed</b>, as <a href=\"https://ianlap.github.io/SigmaTau.jl/dev/reference/kalman/#SigmaTau.simulate_clock\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><code>simulate_clock</code></a> and <a href=\"https://ianlap.github.io/SigmaTau.jl/dev/reference/ensemble/#SigmaTau.simulate_ensemble\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><code>simulate_ensemble</code></a> do. The same seed gives the same records. Several clocks are simulated against one ideal reference, as columns that share their instants.</p>",
      "<p>The clocks are added to those already loaded. One clock opens on the <a href=\"#help/data\">Data</a> page, and two or more open on the <a href=\"#help/ensemble\">Ensemble</a> page, which forms a time scale from them. <b>Run analysis</b> then analyzes them like any other record.</p>",
      "<table><thead><tr><th>Field</th><th>Range</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><b>Samples</b></td><td>A whole number, 64 or more</td></tr><tr><td><b>τ₀ (s)</b></td><td>Above 0</td></tr><tr><td><b>Seed</b></td><td>A whole number, 0 or more</td></tr></tbody></table>",
    ] },
  ] },
  { id: "stability", nav: "Stability", web: "https://ianlap.github.io/SigmaTau.jl/dev/explorer/stability/", sections: [
    { id: "", level: 1, title: "Stability", html: [
      "<h1>Stability</h1>",
      "<p>The Stability page plots the deviations of the last run against the averaging time, on logarithmic axes, and lists their values. The explorer opens on this page.</p>",
    ] },
    { id: "The-plot", level: 2, title: "The plot", html: [
      "<h2>The plot</h2>",
      "<p><b>Frequency σy(τ)</b> and <b>Time σx(τ)</b> switch between the two kinds of estimator: the fractional frequency deviations, which have no unit, and the time deviations, in seconds. The page shows the estimators of one kind at a time. <a href=\"#help/estimators\">Estimators and noise types</a> lists which is which.</p>",
      "<p>With one clock, each estimator is a line in its own color, named in the legend. With several clocks the color is that of the clock and the line style that of the estimator, and the status bar lists the line styles.</p>",
      "<p>The line beside the two buttons states the settings of the <a href=\"#help/panel/Options\">side panel</a>: the confidence level, the τ grid, the bias correction when an estimator that takes one is in the list, and the noise type when it is not identified at each averaging time.</p>",
      "<table><thead><tr><th>Control</th><th>Effect</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><b>Noise ID</b></td><td>Shows the noise type identified at each averaging time in a strip above the plot</td></tr><tr><td><b>CI band</b></td><td>Draws the confidence intervals as a shaded band. Without it they are error bars</td></tr><tr><td><b>Fit</b></td><td>Draws the <a href=\"#help/stability/The-noise-model\">noise model</a> and lists its coefficients</td></tr><tr><td><b>Pin run</b></td><td>Keeps the curves of this run on the plot, as <a href=\"#help/stability/Pinned-runs\">pinned runs</a></td></tr><tr><td>Mask menu</td><td>On the time axis, draws a <a href=\"#help/stability/Masks\">telecom mask</a></td></tr></tbody></table>",
      "<p>Hovering over a point shows its averaging time and value, with the EDF and the noise type behind its interval. MTIE and TIE rms have no interval.</p>",
      "<p><b>Noise ID</b> and <b>CI band</b> need the confidence intervals, since the noise types are identified for them. Both are disabled while <b>Confidence intervals</b> is off in the panel.</p>",
    ] },
    { id: "Noise-identification", level: 2, title: "Noise identification", html: [
      "<h2>Noise identification</h2>",
      "<p>The strip above the plot shows the noise type that the run identified at each averaging time, joined into stretches of one type. It comes from the first estimator in the list that reports noise types, and it is shown on the frequency axis only. <a href=\"#help/estimators/Noise-types\">Noise types</a> explains the labels, and <a href=\"https://ianlap.github.io/SigmaTau.jl/dev/explanation/noise_identification/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Noise identification</a> says how they are found.</p>",
      "<p>A label is what the test reads at that averaging time, and not always a noise that the record holds. The record of the figure above has white PM, white FM and random-walk FM. Its stretches of FPM and FFM lie where two of them cross, and there the test reads the type between them (<a href=\"https://ianlap.github.io/SigmaTau.jl/dev/explanation/noise_identification/#How-far-to-trust-a-label\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">How far to trust a label</a>).</p>",
      "<p>With one clock, a dashed line along each stretch has the slope of that noise type in that estimator, drawn a little above the curve, and dotted vertical lines mark where the type changes. A curve that leaves its guide has a mixture of noises there, or a label that does not fit. With several clocks there is one strip for each clock, outlined in its color, and no guides.</p>",
    ] },
    { id: "The-table", level: 2, title: "The table", html: [
      "<h2>The table</h2>",
      "<p>With one clock, <b>Per-τ results</b> lists the values of the estimator chosen in its menu:</p>",
      "<table><thead><tr><th>Column</th><th>Holds</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><b>τ (s)</b></td><td>The averaging time</td></tr><tr><td><b>σ</b></td><td>The deviation</td></tr><tr><td><b>EDF</b></td><td>The equivalent degrees of freedom of the estimate</td></tr><tr><td><b>noise</b></td><td>The noise type at that averaging time</td></tr></tbody></table>",
      "<p>Hovering over a row shows its <a href=\"https://ianlap.github.io/SigmaTau.jl/dev/explanation/confidence/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">confidence interval</a> and the number of terms in the estimate. Every value is in the TSV and JSON <a href=\"#help/export\">exports</a>.</p>",
    ] },
    { id: "The-noise-model", level: 2, title: "The noise model", html: [
      "<h2>The noise model</h2>",
      "<p><b>Fit</b> draws a power-law model of the noise of the record, <span class=\"math\"><i>S</i><sub><i>y</i></sub>(<i>f</i>) = Σ<sub>α</sub> <i>h</i><sub>α</sub><i>f</i><sup>α</sup></span>, as a dotted line under each curve of ADEV, MDEV, HDEV, MHDEV and TDEV: the deviation that the model gives in that estimator. Under the table, <b>Noise model</b> lists the coefficients <span class=\"math\"><i>h</i><sub>α</sub></span> above zero and the ADEV that the model gives at 1 s, 10 s, 100 s, 1000 s, <span class=\"math\">10<sup>4</sup></span> s and one day, as far as half the span of the record.</p>",
      "<p>The model is fitted to the MHDEV of the record on the octave grid, by nonnegative least squares over the five noise types from white PM to random-walk FM, as <a href=\"https://ianlap.github.io/SigmaTau.jl/dev/reference/spectra/#SigmaTau.powerlaw_fit\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><code>powerlaw_fit</code></a> does. It does not depend on the estimators in the list or on the τ grid. A noise that the record does not have comes out small and positive, so compare the dotted lines with the curves before using a coefficient.</p>",
    ] },
    { id: "A-model-built-by-hand", level: 3, title: "A model built by hand", html: [
      "<h3>A model built by hand</h3>",
      "<p><b>Edit</b> opens a list of components that takes the place of the automatic fit. Choose a noise type, give the span of averaging times over which it dominates the MHDEV, and press <b>Add</b>. An empty field leaves that end of the span open. The level of the noise is fitted over its span to what the other components leave of the MHDEV, and every level is fitted again when a component comes or goes, as <a href=\"https://ianlap.github.io/SigmaTau.jl/dev/reference/spectra/#SigmaTau.add_component!\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><code>add_component!</code></a> does. The explorer runs the analysis after each change.</p>",
      "<p>While the list is open, a dashed gray line shows the MHDEV that the components leave. <b>Undo</b> removes the last component, and <b>Back to the automatic fit</b> removes them all. Each of the seven noise types enters once.</p>",
      "<p>The choice <b>From the fitted model</b> of the option <b>Noise type</b> uses the automatic fit, also when a model has been built by hand.</p>",
    ] },
    { id: "Masks", level: 2, title: "Masks", html: [
      "<h2>Masks</h2>",
      "<p>On the time axis, the menu beside the checkboxes draws the limits of an ITU-T wander mask over MTIE and TDEV. With one clock, a card under the table says how each of the two compares:</p>",
      "<table><thead><tr><th>Text</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><b>passes, 8.6× under</b></td><td>Every value is at or under its limit, the closest by a factor of 8.6</td></tr><tr><td><b>fails, 2.1× over</b></td><td>The worst value is over its limit by a factor of 2.1</td></tr><tr><td><b>no τ in the mask's range</b></td><td>The mask does not cover an averaging time of the run</td></tr></tbody></table>",
      "<table><thead><tr><th>Menu entry</th><th>Mask</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><b>G.811 PRC</b></td><td>Wander generation of a primary reference clock</td></tr><tr><td><b>G.813 SEC opt 1</b>, <b>opt 2</b></td><td>Wander generation of an SDH equipment clock, options 1 and 2</td></tr><tr><td><b>G.8262 EEC opt 1</b>, <b>opt 2</b></td><td>Wander generation of a synchronous equipment clock, options 1 and 2</td></tr><tr><td><b>G.823 PRC</b>, <b>SSU</b>, <b>SEC</b>, <b>PDH interface</b></td><td>Network limits for wander at the four kinds of interface</td></tr></tbody></table>",
      "<p><a href=\"https://ianlap.github.io/SigmaTau.jl/dev/reference/estimators/#SigmaTau.TELECOM_MASKS\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><code>TELECOM_MASKS</code></a> gives the clauses of the recommendations. The recommendations measure MTIE and TDEV through a 10 Hz low-pass filter. The explorer applies no filter and compares the record as it was measured, as <a href=\"https://ianlap.github.io/SigmaTau.jl/dev/reference/estimators/#SigmaTau.mask_check\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><code>mask_check</code></a> does.</p>",
    ] },
    { id: "Pinned-runs", level: 2, title: "Pinned runs", html: [
      "<h2>Pinned runs</h2>",
      "<p><b>Pin run</b> keeps the curves of the last run on the plot, as thin broken lines under those of every later run. Each pin is a chip beside the button, <b>Pin 1</b>, <b>Pin 2</b>, and hovering over it lists what its run was: the estimators, the τ grid, the analysis range and the drift removal. × removes the pin.</p>",
      "<p>A pin shows what a change does: pin, change the drift removal, the range or the bias correction, and run again. Pins are part of the plot and of its image. They are not in the other exports or in a saved session.</p>",
    ] },
    { id: "Several-clocks", level: 2, title: "Several clocks", html: [
      "<h2>Several clocks</h2>",
      "<p>With several clocks the table is <b>Compare at τ</b>: the value of each clock at one averaging time, for the first three estimators of the list on the axis shown. The lowest value in each column is in bold, which marks the most stable clock at that averaging time. A click on the plot moves the averaging time to the nearest one of the run, and a dashed line marks it. The menu of the card does the same.</p>",
    ] },
  ] },
  { id: "spectrum", nav: "Spectrum", web: "https://ianlap.github.io/SigmaTau.jl/dev/explorer/spectrum/", sections: [
    { id: "", level: 1, title: "Spectrum", html: [
      "<h1>Spectrum</h1>",
      "<p>The Spectrum page shows the record in the frequency domain: the spectrum of the fractional frequency, <span class=\"math\"><i>S</i><sub><i>y</i></sub>(<i>f</i>)</span>, above the phase noise <span class=\"math\">ℒ(<i>f</i>)</span> at a carrier frequency or the spectrum of the phase, <span class=\"math\"><i>S</i><sub><i>x</i></sub>(<i>f</i>)</span>. The two plots share the frequency axis, and a zoom in one zooms the other.</p>",
    ] },
    { id: "What-is-computed", level: 2, title: "What is computed", html: [
      "<h2>What is computed</h2>",
      "<p>Every run computes the two spectra of each clock, from the record as prepared on the <a href=\"#help/data\">Data</a> page and within its analysis range. The estimator choice of the panel has no part in them.</p>",
      "<p>Both are one-sided densities by Welch's method, as <a href=\"https://ianlap.github.io/SigmaTau.jl/dev/reference/spectra/#SigmaTau.psd_y\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><code>psd_y</code></a> and <a href=\"https://ianlap.github.io/SigmaTau.jl/dev/reference/spectra/#SigmaTau.psd_x\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><code>psd_x</code></a> compute them: the record is cut into segments that overlap by half, each segment is tapered with a Hann window, and the squared transforms are averaged. The segment is the largest power of two up to a quarter of the record. The frequencies run from the reciprocal of the segment's duration to <span class=\"math\">1/(2τ<sub>0</sub>)</span>.</p>",
      "<table><thead><tr><th>Plot</th><th>Quantity</th><th>Unit</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Upper</td><td><span class=\"math\"><i>S</i><sub><i>y</i></sub>(<i>f</i>)</span>, fractional frequency</td><td>1/Hz</td></tr><tr><td>Lower</td><td><span class=\"math\">ℒ(<i>f</i>)</span> at the carrier</td><td>dBc/Hz</td></tr><tr><td>Lower</td><td><span class=\"math\"><i>S</i><sub><i>x</i></sub>(<i>f</i>)</span>, phase as time</td><td>s²/Hz</td></tr></tbody></table>",
      "<aside class=\"warning\"><h4>The low end of the lower plot</h4><p>The phase spectrum of FM noise is steep, and the taper then leaks power from the lowest frequencies into the bins above them. In simulation the estimate of <span class=\"math\"><i>S</i><sub><i>x</i></sub>(<i>f</i>)</span> is within 2 % from the eighth bin on for white and flicker FM, and on random-walk FM it is still twice too high at the 16th bin (<a href=\"https://ianlap.github.io/SigmaTau.jl/dev/reference/spectra/#SigmaTau.psd_x\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><code>psd_x</code></a>). On a record with such noise, read the low frequencies from <span class=\"math\"><i>S</i><sub><i>y</i></sub>(<i>f</i>)</span>, which is less steep by <span class=\"math\"><i>f</i><sup>2</sup></span>. Its first bin should not be read either (<a href=\"https://ianlap.github.io/SigmaTau.jl/dev/reference/spectra/#SigmaTau.psd_y\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><code>psd_y</code></a>).</p></aside>",
    ] },
    { id: "Settings", level: 2, title: "Settings", html: [
      "<h2>Settings</h2>",
      "<p>The settings change what is shown, and they apply at once, without a run.</p>",
      "<table><thead><tr><th>Setting</th><th>Effect</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><b>Carrier frequency for ℒ(f)</b></td><td>The carrier <span class=\"math\">ν<sub>0</sub></span> in hertz, 10 MHz at first. It takes a number such as <code>10e6</code></td></tr><tr><td><b>Phase noise shown as</b></td><td><b>ℒ(f) dBc/Hz</b> or <b>Sx(f)</b> in the lower plot</td></tr><tr><td><b>Averaging</b></td><td><b>Log-binned</b> shows the mean of the density in ten bins per decade of frequency. <b>Welch, every bin</b> shows the estimate as computed</td></tr><tr><td><b>Show power-law slope guides</b></td><td>Labels the stretches of <span class=\"math\"><i>S</i><sub><i>y</i></sub>(<i>f</i>)</span> whose slope is close to that of a power law</td></tr></tbody></table>",
      "<p>The phase noise follows from the phase spectrum as <span class=\"math\">ℒ(<i>f</i>) = <sup>1</sup>⁄<sub>2</sub>(2πν<sub>0</sub>)<sup>2</sup><i>S</i><sub><i>x</i></sub>(<i>f</i>)</span>, in decibels (<a href=\"https://ianlap.github.io/SigmaTau.jl/dev/bibliography/#ieee1139-2022-definitions\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">IEEE Std 1139-2022, Eqs. (A.13) and (A.15)</a>). The record gives <span class=\"math\"><i>S</i><sub><i>x</i></sub>(<i>f</i>)</span>, and the carrier only scales it: the phase noise of one record reads 20 dB higher at 100 MHz than at 10 MHz.</p>",
    ] },
    { id: "Slope-guides", level: 2, title: "Slope guides", html: [
      "<h2>Slope guides</h2>",
      "<p>With one clock, a label such as <b>f⁰ white FM</b> marks a stretch of <span class=\"math\"><i>S</i><sub><i>y</i></sub>(<i>f</i>)</span> with the slope of one noise type. The log-binned spectrum is cut into three stretches of equal numbers of points, a line is fitted to each on logarithmic axes, and a stretch is labeled when its slope is within 0.35 of a whole number from −2 to 2.</p>",
      "<p>The stretches are fixed, so one that spans two noise types is labeled by its mean slope. The record of the figure above has white PM, white FM and random-walk FM, and no flicker noise: its first and last stretches each span two of them.</p>",
      "<table><thead><tr><th>Slope of <span class=\"math\"><i>S</i><sub><i>y</i></sub>(<i>f</i>)</span></th><th>Noise</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><span class=\"math\"><i>f</i><sup>2</sup></span></td><td>White PM</td></tr><tr><td><span class=\"math\"><i>f</i><sup>1</sup></span></td><td>Flicker PM</td></tr><tr><td><span class=\"math\"><i>f</i><sup>0</sup></span></td><td>White FM</td></tr><tr><td><span class=\"math\"><i>f</i><sup>−1</sup></span></td><td>Flicker FM</td></tr><tr><td><span class=\"math\"><i>f</i><sup>−2</sup></span></td><td>Random-walk FM</td></tr></tbody></table>",
      "<p><a href=\"https://ianlap.github.io/SigmaTau.jl/dev/explanation/noise/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Power-law noise</a> relates these slopes to those of the deviations.</p>",
    ] },
    { id: "Several-clocks", level: 2, title: "Several clocks", html: [
      "<h2>Several clocks</h2>",
      "<p>With several clocks, the spectra are overlaid in the colors of the clocks, the settings move to a bar above the plots, and no slope guides are drawn.</p>",
    ] },
    { id: "Records-with-gaps", level: 2, title: "Records with gaps", html: [
      "<h2>Records with gaps</h2>",
      "<p>The spectra need a record without missing samples. A run analyzes a record with gaps without its spectrum, and the page then shows <b>No spectrum: the record has gaps</b> in place of the plots. Beside other clocks, a line above the plots names the clock that has none. Fill the gaps on the <a href=\"#help/data/Preparing-the-record\">Data</a> page, or choose an analysis range without them.</p>",
    ] },
  ] },
  { id: "dynamic", nav: "Dynamic", web: "https://ianlap.github.io/SigmaTau.jl/dev/explorer/dynamic/", sections: [
    { id: "", level: 1, title: "Dynamic", html: [
      "<h1>Dynamic</h1>",
      "<p>The Dynamic page shows whether the stability of a clock changes during the record. It computes the Allan or the Hadamard deviation in a window that slides along the record and draws the result as a map of time against averaging time (<a href=\"https://ianlap.github.io/SigmaTau.jl/dev/bibliography/#galleani-2009-davar\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Galleani and Tavella (2009)</a>). A single curve of <span class=\"math\">σ<sub><i>y</i></sub>(τ)</span> averages over the whole record. The map shows a step in the noise level, a burst or a disturbed stretch where it happens.</p>",
    ] },
    { id: "Settings", level: 2, title: "Settings", html: [
      "<h2>Settings</h2>",
      "<table><thead><tr><th>Setting</th><th>Effect</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><b>Estimator</b></td><td><b>DADEV</b>, the dynamic Allan deviation of <a href=\"https://ianlap.github.io/SigmaTau.jl/dev/reference/estimators/#SigmaTau.dadev\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><code>dadev</code></a>, or <b>DHDEV</b>, the dynamic Hadamard deviation of <a href=\"https://ianlap.github.io/SigmaTau.jl/dev/reference/estimators/#SigmaTau.dhdev\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><code>dhdev</code></a>, which does not respond to linear frequency drift within a window</td></tr><tr><td><b>Window length</b></td><td>A quarter, an eighth, a sixteenth or a thirty-second of the record, and 16 samples at least. The line below gives the samples in a window and the number of windows</td></tr><tr><td><b>View</b></td><td><b>Map</b>, or <b>3D waterfall</b>, a surface that a drag turns</td></tr></tbody></table>",
      "<p>The window moves by half its length, so a window of one eighth of the record gives 15 windows. The averaging times are those of the τ grid of the <a href=\"#help/panel/Options\">side panel</a> that fit in one window. A shorter window follows changes more closely, reaches shorter averaging times only and scatters more, since each cell rests on the samples of one window.</p>",
      "<p>The page computes its maps when it is shown and again when the record, its preparation, its analysis range or a setting changes. It needs no run, and the estimators of the panel have no part in it. The record must have no missing samples.</p>",
    ] },
    { id: "The-map", level: 2, title: "The map", html: [
      "<h2>The map</h2>",
      "<p>Each row is a window, placed at the time of its center, and each column an averaging time. The color gives the deviation on a logarithmic scale, from the color of the card for the lowest value to the strongest color for the highest.</p>",
      "<p>A clock whose noise does not change shows the same color down each column, within the scatter of the estimate. A change during the record shows as a change down the columns.</p>",
      "<p>Hovering over a cell shows its value, and a click pins it. <b>Selected cell</b> then lists the time of the window's center, the averaging time, the deviation in that window, and under <b>Whole record</b> the ADEV or HDEV of the whole record at that averaging time, from the last run. That line is empty when the estimator was not in the last run or the averaging time not on its grid.</p>",
      "<p>The map carries no confidence intervals. Each row is the deviation of one window of samples, so its interval is that of <a href=\"https://ianlap.github.io/SigmaTau.jl/dev/reference/estimators/#SigmaTau.adev\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><code>adev</code></a> or <a href=\"https://ianlap.github.io/SigmaTau.jl/dev/reference/estimators/#SigmaTau.hdev\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><code>hdev</code></a> on a record of the window's length.</p>",
    ] },
    { id: "Several-clocks", level: 2, title: "Several clocks", html: [
      "<h2>Several clocks</h2>",
      "<p>With several clocks, each has its own map, on one color scale, and the settings move to a bar above the maps. There is no 3D view and no selected cell.</p>",
      "<p><b>Compare at τ</b>, below the maps, plots the deviation of every clock against the time of the window, at one averaging time. A dotted line gives the value of each clock over the whole record, from the last run. The menu of the card or a click on a column of a map changes the averaging time. A clock with another sample interval is shown at its nearest averaging time, within half an octave, which the legend then names.</p>",
    ] },
  ] },
  { id: "holdover", nav: "Holdover", web: "https://ianlap.github.io/SigmaTau.jl/dev/explorer/holdover/", sections: [
    { id: "", level: 1, title: "Holdover", html: [
      "<h1>Holdover</h1>",
      "<p>The Holdover page measures, on the record itself, how far a clock wanders once its reference is gone. A least-squares line through a training stretch of the phase gives the frequency of the clock. The clock then runs on at that frequency from the last reading of the stretch, and the error after a holdover time <span class=\"math\">τ</span> is the phase that the record shows there less the phase predicted. The stretch moves along the record one sample at a time, so each holdover time has many trials. The test is that of <a href=\"https://ianlap.github.io/SigmaTau.jl/dev/reference/holdover/#SigmaTau.holdover\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><code>holdover</code></a>, which <a href=\"https://ianlap.github.io/SigmaTau.jl/dev/reference/holdover/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Holdover</a> describes.</p>",
    ] },
    { id: "Settings", level: 2, title: "Settings", html: [
      "<h2>Settings</h2>",
      "<table><thead><tr><th>Setting</th><th>Effect</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><b>Training stretch</b></td><td><b>1/8</b>, <b>1/4</b> or <b>1/2</b> of the record, or <b>2τ</b>, a stretch twice as long as each holdover time. With the drift learned the last choice is <b>3τ</b>, three times as long. The line below gives the length</td></tr><tr><td><b>Predicted by</b></td><td><b>Least-squares fit</b>, or <b>Kalman filter</b> (<a href=\"#help/holdover/explorer-holdover-kalman\">below</a>)</td></tr><tr><td><b>Learned from the stretch</b></td><td><b>Frequency</b>, from a line, or <b>Frequency and drift</b>, from a parabola</td></tr><tr><td><b>Time error limit</b></td><td>A time error in seconds, such as <code>1e-6</code>. The page then gives how long the clock stays within it. Empty for no limit</td></tr><tr><td><b>Share of trials</b></td><td>68.3 %, 90 %, 95 % or 99 %: the share of the trials whose error the dashed curve bounds</td></tr><tr><td><b>Start from the last reading</b></td><td>On, the clock starts from the last reading of the stretch. Off, it starts from the value of the fit there, which averages white PM down, and on a clock with random-walk FM starts from the misfit of the line</td></tr></tbody></table>",
      "<p>The holdover times are the octave multiples of the sample interval, up to the length of the training stretch and no further than half of what the record has left after it. With <b>2τ</b> or <b>3τ</b> they go as far as leaves half the record for the trials.</p>",
      "<p>The page computes when it is shown and again when the record, its preparation, its analysis range or a setting changes. It needs no run, and the estimators of the panel have no part in it. The record needs 16 samples or more. A trial is left out when missing samples take more than half of its training stretch, or the reading it starts from or is compared with.</p>",
    ] },
    { id: "The-plot", level: 2, title: "The plot", html: [
      "<h2>The plot</h2>",
      "<p>The plot gives the time error against the holdover time, on logarithmic axes.</p>",
      "<table><thead><tr><th>Curve</th><th>Shows</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><b>RMS</b></td><td>The root mean square of the error over the trials</td></tr><tr><td><b>95 % of trials</b>, or the share chosen</td><td>The error that this share of the trials stayed within</td></tr><tr><td><b>Worst trial</b></td><td>The largest error of any trial</td></tr><tr><td><b>Predicted RMS (noise model)</b></td><td>The RMS error that a power-law model of the noise predicts for the same test. The model is fitted to the modified Hadamard deviation of the record, as that of <b>Fit</b> on the <a href=\"#help/stability/The-noise-model\">Stability</a> page. A model built by hand there does not enter</td></tr><tr><td><b>√6·TDEV</b>, or <b>√20·HTDEV</b> with the drift learned</td><td>The RMS error of predicting the next <span class=\"math\">τ</span>-average of the phase from the two before it with a line, or from the three before it with a parabola. This is what the time deviation and the Hadamard time deviation measure</td></tr></tbody></table>",
      "<p>With a limit set, a horizontal line marks it, and a dotted vertical line the holdover time at which the dashed curve reaches it.</p>",
      "<p>The card beside the plot lists the training stretch, the holdover time within the limit, and the same time as the noise model predicts it. Below come the shares of the noise types in the predicted error variance at the longest holdover time, the ratio of the RMS to the reference curve at three holdover times, and a table of the RMS, the chosen share, the worst trial and the number of trials at each holdover time. Where the limit is passed at the first holdover time already, the card says <b>under</b> that time, and where it is not reached at the last, <b>longer than</b> that time.</p>",
    ] },
    { id: "Reading-it", level: 2, title: "Reading it", html: [
      "<h2>Reading it</h2>",
      "<ul><li>The trials overlap, so they are not independent. The worst trial is the worst on this record and not a bound.</li><li>A longer stretch averages white FM down and lets random-walk FM carry the frequency away, so the error has a minimum over the length of the stretch. Trying the three shares of the record shows which side of it the record is on.</li><li>A drift <span class=\"math\"><i>D</i></span> that the line does not learn adds <span class=\"math\"><i>D</i>τ(τ + <i>T</i>)/2</span> to every trial, with <span class=\"math\"><i>T</i></span> the length of the stretch. A parabola learns it, at a price that depends on the noise. In simulation, on clocks without drift, the RMS with the parabola was 1.0 to 9 times that with the line on white FM and 1.0 to 5 times on flicker FM, the more the shorter the stretch against the holdover time. On random-walk FM it was 8 to 24 % lower where the stretch was four times the holdover time or longer.</li><li>With the stretch <b>2τ</b> (<b>3τ</b>), the test is the counterpart, on single readings and a least-squares fit, of the prediction that the thin reference curve measures on <span class=\"math\">τ</span>-averages. The two are equal at the sample interval. In simulation the RMS then runs a factor of 1.2 to 1.4 above the reference wherever FM noise dominates. Where white PM dominates the two part, because the time deviation averages the readings and the test compares single ones.</li><li>The predicted curve holds noise alone. A measured RMS above it at long holdover times points to drift, or to noise that the fitted model lacks.</li></ul>",
    ] },
    { id: "explorer-holdover-kalman", level: 2, title: "The Kalman filter as the predictor", html: [
      "<h2>The Kalman filter as the predictor</h2>",
      "<p>With <b>Kalman filter</b> a <a href=\"https://ianlap.github.io/SigmaTau.jl/dev/reference/kalman/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Kalman filter</a> takes the place of the fit. It passes once over the record, and from every sample past the first training stretch its estimate is carried <span class=\"math\">τ</span> ahead without further readings and compared with the reading there. The filter has read the whole record up to each start, and the stretch sets where the trials begin and what the filter knows of the clock: its <a href=\"https://ianlap.github.io/SigmaTau.jl/dev/explanation/clock_model/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">clock model</a> is fitted to the first training stretch alone, so the noise levels are not learned from the readings that the predictions are compared with. The model keeps the flicker FM and the flicker PM that the fit finds, as <a href=\"https://ianlap.github.io/SigmaTau.jl/dev/explanation/clock_model/#Flicker-states\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Markov states</a>.</p>",
      "<p>The training stretch is then a share of the record, <b>Start from the last reading</b> does not apply, and the holdover times go up to half of what the record has left after the stretch. <b>Frequency and drift</b> gives the model a drift state.</p>",
      "<p>The gray curve is then <b>Claimed by the filter</b>: the standard deviation that the filter itself gives the error. It is dotted past the longest averaging time of the fit that the model comes from, between an eighth and a quarter of the stretch: from there the claim extrapolates the model. The card gives the ratio of the RMS to it at the shortest and the longest holdover time, and the model: the noise of the readings <code>R</code>, white FM <code>q₁</code> and random-walk FM <code>q₂</code>, and the flicker FM <code>h₋₁</code> and the flicker PM <code>h₁</code> where the fit finds them.</p>",
      "<ul><li>Where the model fits the clock the ratio is near 1. Above 1, the clock has more noise at that holdover time than the model.</li><li>The longest holdover times are the least certain. The RMS there rests on few independent trials, and the claim on what one stretch shows of the slow noise. In simulation, on records of 8192 samples with a quarter of the record as the stretch, the ratio at the sample interval was 0.97 to 1.04 in four records out of five, on white FM, on flicker FM, and on white FM that gives way to random-walk FM at 390 s. At 2048 s it was 0.3 to 1.3 on white FM, 0.4 to 1.3 on flicker FM, and 0.5 to 8 on the white and random-walk FM.</li><li>The levels are those of a fit to one stretch, which can find a noise type that the clock does not have. In the same simulation the model of 79 of the 200 white FM records held some flicker FM, and that of 48 some random-walk FM.</li></ul>",
    ] },
    { id: "Several-clocks", level: 2, title: "Several clocks", html: [
      "<h2>Several clocks</h2>",
      "<p>With several clocks, the settings but the last move to a bar above the plot, and each clock has one color: its RMS as a solid line and the chosen share of its trials as a dashed one. The worst trial, the prediction and the reference curve are not drawn. The card lists, for each clock, the time within the limit and the RMS at its longest holdover time.</p>",
    ] },
  ] },
  { id: "steering", nav: "Steering", web: "https://ianlap.github.io/SigmaTau.jl/dev/explorer/steering/", sections: [
    { id: "", level: 1, title: "Steering", html: [
      "<h1>Steering</h1>",
      "<p>The Steering page replays a steering loop on the record. The record is the clock running free against its reference, and the page shows what the loop would have made of it. A <a href=\"https://ianlap.github.io/SigmaTau.jl/dev/reference/kalman/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Kalman filter</a> estimates the phase and the frequency of the clock from the readings, and each steer changes the frequency of the clock by a gain times each estimate. The loop is that of <a href=\"https://ianlap.github.io/SigmaTau.jl/dev/reference/steering/#SigmaTau.steer\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><code>steer</code></a>, which <a href=\"https://ianlap.github.io/SigmaTau.jl/dev/reference/steering/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Steering</a> describes.</p>",
    ] },
    { id: "Settings", level: 2, title: "Settings", html: [
      "<h2>Settings</h2>",
      "<table><thead><tr><th>Setting</th><th>Effect</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><b>Gains from</b></td><td><b>Time constant</b>, the gains of a critically damped loop with that time constant, by pole placement, or <b>Cost</b>, the gains that minimize a weighted sum of phase², frequency² and steer²</td></tr><tr><td><b>Time constant</b></td><td>In seconds. Empty, it is 1/128 of the record, the value that the field shows in gray</td></tr><tr><td><b>Integral gain</b></td><td>A third gain, on the running sum of the estimated phase</td></tr><tr><td><b>Weights on</b></td><td>With <b>Cost</b>, the weights on <b>phase²</b>, <b>frequency²</b> and <b>steer²</b>. Those on phase² and steer² are above 0. A larger weight on steer² gives a slower loop with smaller steers</td></tr><tr><td><b>Steer every</b></td><td>A steer at every sample, or at every 2 to 64 samples. The gains are worked out for that interval</td></tr><tr><td><b>Clock model from the first</b></td><td><b>1/8</b>, <b>1/4</b> or <b>1/2</b> of the record, the stretch to which the clock model of the filter is fitted. The line below gives its length</td></tr><tr><td><b>Drift state</b></td><td>The model also holds a frequency drift, which the loop steers out ahead</td></tr><tr><td><b>Plot</b></td><td><b>σ(τ)</b> or <b>Record</b> (<a href=\"#help/steering/explorer-steering-plot\">below</a>)</td></tr></tbody></table>",
      "<p>The <a href=\"https://ianlap.github.io/SigmaTau.jl/dev/explanation/clock_model/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">clock model</a> is fitted to the first stretch alone and keeps the flicker FM and the flicker PM that the fit finds, as that of the <a href=\"#help/holdover/explorer-holdover-kalman\">Kalman filter</a> on the Holdover page. The loop runs over the whole record, and every result counts from the end of that stretch, so the loop is not judged on readings that its model was learned from.</p>",
      "<p>The page computes when it is shown and again when the record, its preparation, its analysis range or a setting changes. It needs no run. Of the side panel it takes the τ grid alone. The record needs 64 samples or more.</p>",
    ] },
    { id: "explorer-steering-plot", level: 2, title: "The plot", html: [
      "<h2>The plot</h2>",
      "<p>With <b>σ(τ)</b> the plot gives the Allan deviation against the averaging time, on logarithmic axes.</p>",
      "<table><thead><tr><th>Curve</th><th>Shows</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><b>Free-running</b></td><td>The Allan deviation of the record</td></tr><tr><td><b>Steered</b></td><td>That of the steered record</td></tr><tr><td><b>Predicted (clock model)</b></td><td>That of the readings of the settled loop as the clock model gives it, the <a href=\"https://ianlap.github.io/SigmaTau.jl/dev/reference/steering/#SigmaTau.SteeringPrediction\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><code>SteeringPrediction</code></a> of <code>steer(model, gains; readings=true)</code>. It is drawn for a steer at every sample</td></tr></tbody></table>",
      "<p>A dotted vertical line marks the longest time constant of the loop.</p>",
      "<p>With <b>Record</b> the plot gives the steered phase over the whole record, and on the right axis the frequency correction in force. A dotted line marks the end of the stretch that the model was fitted to.</p>",
      "<p>The card beside the plot lists the phase gain, the frequency gain and the integral gain if there is one, the time constants of the loop, which come from its poles, and how often it steers. Where the poles are complex, <b>Rings with period</b> gives the period of the overshoot. Below come the RMS of the steered phase and of the steers, each with the value that the model predicts in parentheses, the model itself, and a table of the three deviations at each averaging time.</p>",
    ] },
    { id: "Reading-it", level: 2, title: "Reading it", html: [
      "<h2>Reading it</h2>",
      "<ul><li>Well below the time constant the steered clock follows the free-running one. Well above it the steered deviation falls as <span class=\"math\">1/τ</span>, because the phase is held to the reference.</li><li>A shorter time constant holds the phase closer and steers harder. On the simulated record that the explorer starts with, time constants of 32, 128 and 512 s gave a steered phase of 7, 21 and 91 ps RMS, and steers of 4.9, 1.5 and 0.65 parts in 10¹⁵ RMS.</li><li>A steered curve above the predicted one says that the clock had more noise there than the model fitted to the first stretch.</li><li>Gains from a cost with no weight on frequency² give a loop that rings a little, and the card gives the period.</li><li>A frequency drift that the model does not hold leaves the steered phase off zero. <b>Drift state</b> removes the offset. An integral gain centers the filter's estimate of the phase and not the phase itself, as <a href=\"https://ianlap.github.io/SigmaTau.jl/dev/reference/steering/#The-integral-gain\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Steering</a> explains.</li></ul>",
    ] },
    { id: "Several-clocks", level: 2, title: "Several clocks", html: [
      "<h2>Several clocks</h2>",
      "<p>With several clocks, the settings move to a bar above the plot, and each clock has one color. With <b>σ(τ)</b> its steered deviation is a solid line and its free-running one a dashed line, and the prediction is not drawn. With <b>Record</b> the plot gives the steered phase of each clock. The card lists the RMS of the steered phase and of the steers of each clock.</p>",
    ] },
  ] },
  { id: "ensemble", nav: "Ensemble", web: "https://ianlap.github.io/SigmaTau.jl/dev/explorer/ensemble/", sections: [
    { id: "", level: 1, title: "Ensemble", html: [
      "<h1>Ensemble</h1>",
      "<p>The Ensemble page forms a time scale from a group of clocks that were measured against one reference: a weighted combination of the clocks that is meant to be more stable than any one of them. It shows the stability of the scale, the stability of each clock against it and the weight of each clock.</p>",
    ] },
    { id: "The-clocks", level: 2, title: "The clocks", html: [
      "<h2>The clocks</h2>",
      "<p>The page takes its clocks from the first of these that it finds:</p>",
      "<ol><li>the clock columns of one loaded file of plain columns, two or more, read at the same instants against the same reference;</li><li>clocks simulated together on the <a href=\"#help/data/Simulated-records\">Data</a> or <a href=\"#help/synthesis\">Synthesis</a> page;</li><li>four simulated clocks of its own, of 16384 samples at 1 s.</li></ol>",
      "<p>A note in the side panel says which clocks are in use. A clock chosen as the <b>Reference</b> in the list of clocks is left out of the scale, and so is a clock removed with ×. A hidden clock stays in.</p>",
      "<p>The scale is formed from the columns as the <a href=\"#help/data/The-import-dialog\">import dialog</a> reads them. The drift removal, the gap filling, the outlier removal and the analysis range of the Data page do not apply here.</p>",
      "<p>Where phase columns have missing samples, AT1, AT2 and EWFA form the scale without the clock at those samples (<a href=\"https://ianlap.github.io/SigmaTau.jl/dev/reference/ensemble/#Clocks-that-drop-out-and-return\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Clocks that drop out and return</a>). Greenhall, Jones–Tryon and Stein first fit a model to each clock, which needs a file without missing samples. Frequency columns with missing samples cannot be read.</p>",
    ] },
    { id: "Settings", level: 2, title: "Settings", html: [
      "<h2>Settings</h2>",
      "<table><thead><tr><th>Setting</th><th>Algorithms</th><th>Effect</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><b>Algorithm</b></td><td></td><td>One of the six below</td></tr><tr><td><b>Pivot clock</b></td><td>Greenhall, Jones–Tryon, Stein</td><td>The clock at which the scale is read</td></tr><tr><td><b>Largest weight</b></td><td>AT1, AT2, Stein</td><td>The largest weight one clock may have, from <span class=\"math\">1/<i>k</i></span> for <span class=\"math\"><i>k</i></span> clocks to 1, which is no limit</td></tr><tr><td><b>Frequency steps</b></td><td>AT2</td><td><b>Off</b>, <b>Best</b> or <b>Real</b>: whether the scale looks for frequency steps of a clock, and what it does on finding one</td></tr><tr><td><b>Flicker states</b></td><td>Greenhall, Jones–Tryon</td><td><b>Off</b> or <b>On</b>: whether the model of each clock keeps its flicker FM and flicker PM</td></tr></tbody></table>",
      "<table><thead><tr><th>Algorithm</th><th>Scale</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><b>AT1</b></td><td>Weighs each clock by the inverse of the variance of its prediction error, which the scale estimates as it runs (<a href=\"https://ianlap.github.io/SigmaTau.jl/dev/bibliography/#weiss-weissert-1991-at2\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Weiss and Weissert (1991)</a>)</td></tr><tr><td><b>AT2</b></td><td>AT1 with the frequency of each clock followed by a Kalman filter, from the same paper. With <b>Frequency steps</b>, a clock whose frequency steps is left out for a time. <b>Best</b> forms the scale again from the step on, and <b>Real</b> keeps the values already issued and corrects those that follow</td></tr><tr><td><b>EWFA</b></td><td>Weighs every clock the same (<a href=\"https://ianlap.github.io/SigmaTau.jl/dev/bibliography/#kettering-2023-clock-ensemble\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Kettering (2023), §3.1</a>)</td></tr><tr><td><b>Greenhall</b></td><td>One Kalman filter over all the clocks, with the covariance reduction of <a href=\"https://ianlap.github.io/SigmaTau.jl/dev/bibliography/#greenhall-2003-jones-tryon\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Greenhall (2003)</a>, read at the pivot clock</td></tr><tr><td><b>Jones–Tryon</b></td><td>The same filter without the reduction (<a href=\"https://ianlap.github.io/SigmaTau.jl/dev/bibliography/#jones-tryon-1983-estimating-time\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Jones and Tryon (1983)</a>). It defines no weights</td></tr><tr><td><b>Stein</b></td><td>The scale of <a href=\"https://ianlap.github.io/SigmaTau.jl/dev/bibliography/#stein-2003-demystified\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Stein (2003)</a>, in the form that <a href=\"https://ianlap.github.io/SigmaTau.jl/dev/bibliography/#levine-2012-statistical-modeling\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Levine (2012)</a> describes as KAS-2: a Kalman filter on the difference of each clock from the pivot, with weights for phase, frequency and aging. The weights shown are those of the phase</td></tr></tbody></table>",
      "<p><a href=\"https://ianlap.github.io/SigmaTau.jl/dev/reference/ensemble/#SigmaTau.timescale\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><code>timescale</code></a> describes each algorithm, its equations and where it departs from its source. The three Kalman scales need three or more clocks. They fit a model to each clock from the comparisons alone, as <a href=\"https://ianlap.github.io/SigmaTau.jl/dev/reference/ensemble/#SigmaTau.clock_models\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><code>clock_models</code></a> does.</p>",
      "<p>The model of a clock has white FM and random-walk FM, with the PM as the noise of its readings. With <b>Flicker states</b> off, flicker PM counts as noise of the readings too, and flicker FM is not fitted. With them on, flicker FM is fitted as well, and the model keeps the flicker FM and the flicker PM it finds as <a href=\"https://ianlap.github.io/SigmaTau.jl/dev/explanation/clock_model/#Flicker-states\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Markov states</a>, over the averaging times at which each matters. The levels are those that the fit finds, and on a short record it can find flicker in a clock that has none: of four simulated clocks of white and random-walk FM, 2048 samples long, one got two flicker FM states.</p>",
      "<p>The page forms the scale when it is shown and again when the clocks or a setting change. It needs no run.</p>",
    ] },
    { id: "What-is-shown", level: 2, title: "What is shown", html: [
      "<h2>What is shown</h2>",
      "<p>The upper plot gives the Allan deviation of the scale against the reference, as a solid line, and that of each clock against the scale, as dotted lines. The line above it names the algorithm, the number of samples, the sample interval and the reference, and says when flicker states are on.</p>",
      "<p>With simulated clocks the reference is ideal, and the solid line is the stability of the scale itself. With measured clocks it holds the noise of the reference as well, and it shows the scale only where the reference is the more stable of the two.</p>",
      "<p><b>Weights</b> plots the weight of each clock against time. The table gives, for each clock:</p>",
      "<table><thead><tr><th>Column</th><th>Holds</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><b>Weight</b></td><td>The weight at the end of the record</td></tr><tr><td><b>Mean</b></td><td>The mean weight over the second half of the record</td></tr><tr><td><b>RMS error</b></td><td>The rms prediction error over the second half, in seconds</td></tr></tbody></table>",
      "<p>The Jones–Tryon scale has no weights: the plot of the weights is not shown, and the two weight columns are empty.</p>",
      "<p>With <b>Flicker states</b> on, the note below the table gives the number of states in the model of each clock, as <code>2 FM + 1 PM</code>, or says that no clock has flicker that needs them.</p>",
    ] },
  ] },
  { id: "live", nav: "Live", web: "https://ianlap.github.io/SigmaTau.jl/dev/explorer/live/", sections: [
    { id: "", level: 1, title: "Live", html: [
      "<h1>Live</h1>",
      "<p>The Live page follows a measurement while it runs. It reads one reading at a time from an instrument or from a simulator and updates the deviations with each one, so that the curve fills in toward long averaging times as the record grows.</p>",
    ] },
    { id: "The-source", level: 2, title: "The source", html: [
      "<h2>The source</h2>",
      "<table><thead><tr><th>Source</th><th>Reads from</th><th>Fields</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><b>Simulator</b></td><td>A simulated clock with white PM, white FM and random-walk FM, at one reading per sample interval</td><td>None</td></tr><tr><td><b>Network</b></td><td>The SCPI socket of an instrument on the network, as an LXI instrument offers, on port 5025 as a rule</td><td><b>Address</b>, as <code>host:port</code>, and <b>Query</b></td></tr><tr><td><b>Serial / file</b></td><td>A serial port, or a file of readings, which is read to its end at once</td><td><b>Path</b> and <b>Query</b></td></tr></tbody></table>",
      "<p>The <b>Query</b> is sent before every reading. For the network it is <code>READ?</code> at first. Leave it empty for an instrument that sends readings by itself.</p>",
      "<p>A serial port has to be set up before the measurement starts, since the explorer opens it as a file. On Linux, <code>stty -F /dev/ttyUSB0 9600 raw</code> sets the line speed, and on macOS <code>stty -f</code> does.</p>",
      "<p>From a browser, the serial port and the file are those of the computer that runs <a href=\"#help/install/In-a-browser,-from-the-repository\"><code>serve.py</code></a>, which serves them only on its default address.</p>",
    ] },
    { id: "The-readings", level: 2, title: "The readings", html: [
      "<h2>The readings</h2>",
      "<p>A reading is the first number on a line. Other fields on the line, after a comma, a semicolon or a blank, are ignored.</p>",
      "<table><thead><tr><th>Setting</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><b>Readings</b></td><td><b>Counter frequency (Hz)</b>, <b>Fractional frequency</b> or <b>Phase (s)</b>. The simulator gives phase</td></tr><tr><td><b>Nominal (Hz)</b></td><td>The frequency that counter readings are measured against. Left empty, it is the first reading rounded to four significant digits</td></tr><tr><td><b>τ₀ (s)</b></td><td>The time from one reading to the next, such as the gate time of the counter</td></tr><tr><td><b>Estimators</b></td><td>Any of ADEV, MDEV, HDEV and MHDEV</td></tr></tbody></table>",
      "<p>A counter reading <span class=\"math\">ν</span> becomes the fractional frequency <span class=\"math\"><i>y</i> = ν/ν<sub>0</sub> − 1</span> against the nominal frequency <span class=\"math\">ν<sub>0</sub></span>. A nominal value that is off by a little adds a constant frequency offset, which none of the four deviations responds to.</p>",
      "<p>The sample interval is not measured: set it to the time from one reading to the next. Frequency readings are taken as averages over that whole time. Dead time between the readings is not corrected for (<a href=\"https://ianlap.github.io/SigmaTau.jl/dev/guide/choosing/#Counter-readings\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Counter readings</a>).</p>",
      "<p>A measurement cannot skip a reading. When an instrument answers with the SCPI value for no measurement, <code>9.91E+37</code>, or with a line that does not start with a number, the measurement stops with that as its error.</p>",
    ] },
    { id: "While-it-runs", level: 2, title: "While it runs", html: [
      "<h2>While it runs</h2>",
      "<p><b>Start</b> begins the measurement and <b>Stop</b> ends it. The bar at the top gives the state, the number of readings, the time they span and the last reading. The two plots are updated about four times a second:</p>",
      "<table><thead><tr><th>Plot</th><th>Shows</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><b>σ(τ) so far</b></td><td>The chosen deviations of all the readings so far, on the octave grid</td></tr><tr><td><b>Fractional frequency</b></td><td>The fractional frequency of the last 5000 readings</td></tr></tbody></table>",
      "<p>The deviations are those of <a href=\"https://ianlap.github.io/SigmaTau.jl/dev/reference/estimators/#SigmaTau.adev\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><code>adev</code></a>, <a href=\"https://ianlap.github.io/SigmaTau.jl/dev/reference/estimators/#SigmaTau.mdev\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><code>mdev</code></a>, <a href=\"https://ianlap.github.io/SigmaTau.jl/dev/reference/estimators/#SigmaTau.hdev\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><code>hdev</code></a> and <a href=\"https://ianlap.github.io/SigmaTau.jl/dev/reference/estimators/#SigmaTau.mhdev\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><code>mhdev</code></a> on the readings so far, updated with running sums as <a href=\"https://ianlap.github.io/SigmaTau.jl/dev/reference/suites/#SigmaTau.StreamingStability\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><code>StreamingStability</code></a> keeps them (<a href=\"https://ianlap.github.io/SigmaTau.jl/dev/bibliography/#dobrogowski-2007-realtime-adev\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Dobrogowski and Kasznia (2007)</a>). They carry no confidence intervals and no noise types. Hovering over a point shows the number of terms behind it. The points at the longest averaging times rest on few terms and move the most.</p>",
    ] },
    { id: "Afterwards", level: 2, title: "Afterwards", html: [
      "<h2>Afterwards</h2>",
      "<table><thead><tr><th>Button</th><th>Action</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><b>Save readings</b></td><td>Saves the readings as a text file, one per line, under a line that states the record type, the sample interval and the source. Counter readings are saved as fractional frequency</td></tr><tr><td><b>Analyze readings</b></td><td>Loads the readings as a new clock, opens the <a href=\"#help/stability\">Stability</a> page and runs the analysis, with confidence intervals, noise types and every estimator of the list. It needs a stopped measurement of 16 readings or more</td></tr></tbody></table>",
    ] },
  ] },
  { id: "export", nav: "Exports, sessions and reports", web: "https://ianlap.github.io/SigmaTau.jl/dev/explorer/export/", sections: [
    { id: "", level: 1, title: "Exports, sessions and reports", html: [
      "<h1>Exports, sessions and reports</h1>",
      "<p>The <b>File</b> menu saves the plots on screen, the numbers of the last run, a report of the analysis and the whole session. In a browser each file is a download. The desktop app asks where to save it.</p>",
      "<table><thead><tr><th>Entry</th><th>File</th><th>Holds</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><b>Plot as PNG</b></td><td><code>sigmatau-&lt;page&gt;.png</code></td><td>The plots of the page, at twice their size on screen</td></tr><tr><td><b>Plot as SVG</b></td><td><code>sigmatau-&lt;page&gt;.svg</code></td><td>The same plots as vector graphics</td></tr><tr><td><b>Results as TSV</b></td><td><code>sigmatau-results.tsv</code></td><td>The deviations of the last run, one line for each averaging time</td></tr><tr><td><b>Results as JSON</b></td><td><code>sigmatau-results.json</code></td><td>Everything the last run returned</td></tr><tr><td><b>Report (HTML, prints to PDF)</b></td><td><code>sigmatau-report.html</code></td><td>The analysis as one page that can be printed</td></tr><tr><td><b>Save session…</b></td><td><code>sigmatau-session.json</code></td><td>The clocks, their files and the settings</td></tr><tr><td><b>Open session…</b></td><td></td><td>Opens a saved session</td></tr></tbody></table>",
    ] },
    { id: "Plots", level: 2, title: "Plots", html: [
      "<h2>Plots</h2>",
      "<p>The image is the plot as it is on screen, with its zoom and with any pinned runs. In the light theme the margin around the axes is transparent. In the dark theme the image keeps the dark card behind the plot.</p>",
      "<p>On a page with several plots, such as the two spectra of the Spectrum page or the maps of several clocks on the Dynamic page, one image holds every plot that is shown, placed as on screen. In a browser, the camera button that appears on hovering over a plot saves that plot alone as a PNG.</p>",
    ] },
    { id: "Results", level: 2, title: "Results", html: [
      "<h2>Results</h2>",
      "<p>The TSV file is a table for a spreadsheet or a script, with tabs between the columns:</p>",
      "<table><thead><tr><th>Column</th><th>Holds</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>clock</code></td><td>The name of the clock</td></tr><tr><td><code>estimator</code></td><td>The estimator, such as <code>adev</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>tau_s</code></td><td>The averaging time in seconds</td></tr><tr><td><code>sigma</code></td><td>The deviation</td></tr><tr><td><code>ci_lo</code>, <code>ci_hi</code></td><td>The limits of the confidence interval. Empty without intervals</td></tr><tr><td><code>edf</code></td><td>The equivalent degrees of freedom</td></tr><tr><td><code>noise</code></td><td>The noise type under its name in the package, such as <code>WHFM</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>neff</code></td><td>The number of terms in the estimate</td></tr></tbody></table>",
      "<p>The JSON file is the output of <a href=\"https://ianlap.github.io/SigmaTau.jl/dev/reference/cli/#JSON\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><code>sigmatau analyze</code></a> for each clock, with the name of the clock added: the record check, the settings of the run, the deviations, the spectra, the noise model and the mask checks. With several clocks it is a list.</p>",
      "<p>Both files hold every clock with results, hidden clocks included.</p>",
    ] },
    { id: "The-report", level: 2, title: "The report", html: [
      "<h2>The report</h2>",
      "<p>The report is a single HTML file with its figures inside, which a browser prints to PDF. It records what was analyzed and how:</p>",
      "<table><thead><tr><th>Section</th><th>Holds</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Analysis</td><td>The estimators, the τ grid and limits, the confidence level, the bias correction, the noise type and the reference clock</td></tr><tr><td>Records</td><td>For each clock: the file and its SHA-256 hash, the record type, the samples, the sample interval, the span, the gaps, the outliers, the drift and the preparation</td></tr><tr><td>Stability</td><td>The plots of the Stability page for the frequency and the time estimators, on a white background</td></tr><tr><td>Results</td><td>A table for each clock and estimator: averaging time, deviation, interval, EDF, noise type and number of terms</td></tr><tr><td>Reproduce</td><td>The <code>sigmatau analyze</code> command of each clock</td></tr></tbody></table>",
      "<p>The report states the settings on screen, so it is refused while the results are out of date: run the analysis first. Hidden clocks are left out.</p>",
      "<p>The commands under Reproduce name each file without its folder and print CSV. Run in the folder of the file with the same version of the program, they return the deviations of the report. The hash identifies the file that was analyzed. A gzip file is named and hashed as unpacked, the form in which the program reads it.</p>",
    ] },
    { id: "Sessions", level: 2, title: "Sessions", html: [
      "<h2>Sessions</h2>",
      "<p><b>Save session…</b> writes one JSON file that holds:</p>",
      "<ul><li>every clock, with the content of its file, its import settings, its preparation, its analysis range and its noise model if built by hand;</li><li>the reference clock;</li><li>the estimators and options of the side panel;</li><li>the display settings of the Stability, Spectrum and Dynamic pages and the settings of the Holdover and Steering pages.</li></ul>",
      "<p>It holds no results. <b>Open session…</b>, or the file dropped on the window, puts these clocks and settings in the place of those on screen and runs the analysis. Pinned runs and the settings of the Synthesis, Ensemble and Live pages are not saved.</p>",
      "<p>A session file contains the records themselves. It is as large as they are, and it hands the whole analysis to whoever receives it.</p>",
    ] },
  ] },
  { id: "estimators", nav: "Estimators and noise types", web: "https://ianlap.github.io/SigmaTau.jl/dev/explorer/estimators/", sections: [
    { id: "", level: 1, title: "Estimators and noise types", html: [
      "<h1>Estimators and noise types</h1>",
      "<p>This page is a short reference for the names the explorer shows. The Background pages give the definitions and their sources, and <a href=\"https://ianlap.github.io/SigmaTau.jl/dev/guide/choosing/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Choosing an estimator</a> says which estimator answers which question.</p>",
    ] },
    { id: "Estimators", level: 2, title: "Estimators", html: [
      "<h2>Estimators</h2>",
      "<p>Every estimator works on the phase of the record, at averaging times <span class=\"math\">τ = <i>m</i>τ<sub>0</sub></span> from the τ grid of the <a href=\"#help/panel/Options\">side panel</a>. A frequency record is integrated to phase first.</p>",
      "<p>The frequency estimators give <span class=\"math\">σ<sub><i>y</i></sub>(τ)</span>, without unit:</p>",
      "<table><thead><tr><th>Estimator</th><th>Name</th><th>What it gives</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>ADEV</td><td>Overlapping Allan deviation, <a href=\"https://ianlap.github.io/SigmaTau.jl/dev/reference/estimators/#SigmaTau.adev\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><code>adev</code></a></td><td>The standard measure of frequency stability</td></tr><tr><td>MDEV</td><td>Modified Allan deviation, <a href=\"https://ianlap.github.io/SigmaTau.jl/dev/reference/estimators/#SigmaTau.mdev\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><code>mdev</code></a></td><td>Separates white PM from flicker PM, which ADEV does not</td></tr><tr><td>HDEV</td><td>Overlapping Hadamard deviation, <a href=\"https://ianlap.github.io/SigmaTau.jl/dev/reference/estimators/#SigmaTau.hdev\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><code>hdev</code></a></td><td>Does not respond to linear frequency drift</td></tr><tr><td>MHDEV</td><td>Modified Hadamard deviation, <a href=\"https://ianlap.github.io/SigmaTau.jl/dev/reference/estimators/#SigmaTau.mhdev\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><code>mhdev</code></a></td><td>Separates the two phase noises and does not respond to drift</td></tr><tr><td>PDEV</td><td>Parabolic deviation, <a href=\"https://ianlap.github.io/SigmaTau.jl/dev/reference/estimators/#SigmaTau.pdev\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><code>pdev</code></a></td><td>The deviation of the readings of an Ω counter. It separates the two phase noises as MDEV does</td></tr><tr><td>TOTDEV</td><td>Total deviation, <a href=\"https://ianlap.github.io/SigmaTau.jl/dev/reference/estimators/#SigmaTau.totdev\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><code>totdev</code></a></td><td>ADEV with more degrees of freedom at long averaging times</td></tr><tr><td>MTOTDEV</td><td>Modified total deviation, <a href=\"https://ianlap.github.io/SigmaTau.jl/dev/reference/estimators/#SigmaTau.mtotdev\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><code>mtotdev</code></a></td><td>The total form of MDEV</td></tr><tr><td>HTOTDEV</td><td>Hadamard total deviation, <a href=\"https://ianlap.github.io/SigmaTau.jl/dev/reference/estimators/#SigmaTau.htotdev\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><code>htotdev</code></a></td><td>The total form of HDEV</td></tr><tr><td>MHTOTDEV</td><td>Modified Hadamard total deviation, <a href=\"https://ianlap.github.io/SigmaTau.jl/dev/reference/estimators/#SigmaTau.mhtotdev\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><code>mhtotdev</code></a></td><td>The total form of MHDEV</td></tr><tr><td>THEO1</td><td>Thêo1, <a href=\"https://ianlap.github.io/SigmaTau.jl/dev/reference/estimators/#SigmaTau.theo1\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><code>theo1</code></a></td><td>An estimate of ADEV out to three quarters of the record</td></tr><tr><td>THEOH</td><td>ThêoH, <a href=\"https://ianlap.github.io/SigmaTau.jl/dev/reference/estimators/#SigmaTau.theoh\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><code>theoh</code></a></td><td>ADEV at short averaging times joined to Thêo1, with its bias removed, at long ones</td></tr></tbody></table>",
      "<p>The time estimators give <span class=\"math\">σ<sub><i>x</i></sub>(τ)</span>, in seconds:</p>",
      "<table><thead><tr><th>Estimator</th><th>Name</th><th>What it gives</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>TDEV</td><td>Time deviation, <a href=\"https://ianlap.github.io/SigmaTau.jl/dev/reference/estimators/#SigmaTau.tdev\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><code>tdev</code></a></td><td>MDEV times <span class=\"math\">τ/√<span class=\"rad\">3</span></span></td></tr><tr><td>HTDEV</td><td>Hadamard time deviation, <a href=\"https://ianlap.github.io/SigmaTau.jl/dev/reference/estimators/#SigmaTau.htdev\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><code>htdev</code></a></td><td>MHDEV times <span class=\"math\">τ/√<span class=\"rad\">10/3</span></span>, a statistic defined in this package</td></tr><tr><td>TTOTDEV</td><td>Time total deviation, <a href=\"https://ianlap.github.io/SigmaTau.jl/dev/reference/estimators/#SigmaTau.ttotdev\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><code>ttotdev</code></a></td><td>MTOTDEV times <span class=\"math\">τ/√<span class=\"rad\">3</span></span></td></tr><tr><td>MTIE</td><td>Maximum time interval error, <a href=\"https://ianlap.github.io/SigmaTau.jl/dev/reference/estimators/#SigmaTau.mtie\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><code>mtie</code></a></td><td>The largest peak-to-peak time error within any interval of length <span class=\"math\">τ</span></td></tr><tr><td>TIE rms</td><td>Rms time interval error, <a href=\"https://ianlap.github.io/SigmaTau.jl/dev/reference/estimators/#SigmaTau.tierms\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><code>tierms</code></a></td><td>The rms change of the time error over <span class=\"math\">τ</span></td></tr></tbody></table>",
      "<p>ADEV, MDEV, HDEV, MHDEV, TDEV, HTDEV, MTIE and TIE rms leave out the terms that touch a missing sample. The other estimators need a record without gaps.</p>",
      "<p>MTOTDEV, HTOTDEV, MHTOTDEV, TTOTDEV and the two Thêo estimators take seconds on a record of <span class=\"math\">10<sup>5</sup></span> samples. The others take a fraction of a second.</p>",
    ] },
    { id: "Noise-types", level: 2, title: "Noise types", html: [
      "<h2>Noise types</h2>",
      "<p>Clock noise is modeled as a sum of power laws, <span class=\"math\"><i>S</i><sub><i>y</i></sub>(<i>f</i>) = Σ<sub>α</sub> <i>h</i><sub>α</sub><i>f</i><sup>α</sup></span>. The label at an averaging time names the term that dominates there. PM stands for phase modulation and FM for frequency modulation:</p>",
      "<table><thead><tr><th>Label</th><th>In exports</th><th>Noise</th><th class=\"r\"><span class=\"math\">α</span></th><th>ADEV</th><th>MDEV</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>WPM</td><td><code>WHPM</code></td><td>White PM</td><td class=\"r\">2</td><td><span class=\"math\">τ<sup>−1</sup></span></td><td><span class=\"math\">τ<sup>−3/2</sup></span></td></tr><tr><td>FPM</td><td><code>FLPM</code></td><td>Flicker PM</td><td class=\"r\">1</td><td><span class=\"math\">τ<sup>−1</sup></span>, nearly</td><td><span class=\"math\">τ<sup>−1</sup></span></td></tr><tr><td>WFM</td><td><code>WHFM</code></td><td>White FM</td><td class=\"r\">0</td><td><span class=\"math\">τ<sup>−1/2</sup></span></td><td><span class=\"math\">τ<sup>−1/2</sup></span></td></tr><tr><td>FFM</td><td><code>FLFM</code></td><td>Flicker FM</td><td class=\"r\">−1</td><td><span class=\"math\">τ<sup>0</sup></span></td><td><span class=\"math\">τ<sup>0</sup></span></td></tr><tr><td>RWFM</td><td><code>RWFM</code></td><td>Random-walk FM</td><td class=\"r\">−2</td><td><span class=\"math\">τ<sup>1/2</sup></span></td><td><span class=\"math\">τ<sup>1/2</sup></span></td></tr><tr><td>FWFM</td><td><code>FWFM</code></td><td>Flicker-walk FM</td><td class=\"r\">−3</td><td>none</td><td>none</td></tr><tr><td>RRFM</td><td><code>RRFM</code></td><td>Random-run FM</td><td class=\"r\">−4</td><td>none</td><td>none</td></tr></tbody></table>",
      "<p>The last two columns give the slope of the deviation on logarithmic axes. HDEV follows ADEV, MHDEV and PDEV follow MDEV, and the total and Thêo estimators follow the deviation they extend. The Allan variances do not converge for the last two noise types. The Hadamard family does, with the slopes <span class=\"math\">τ<sup>1</sup></span> and <span class=\"math\">τ<sup>3/2</sup></span>, and these two labels appear for it alone. <a href=\"https://ianlap.github.io/SigmaTau.jl/dev/explanation/noise/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Power-law noise</a> has the full table.</p>",
      "<p>The label comes from the <a href=\"https://ianlap.github.io/SigmaTau.jl/dev/explanation/noise_identification/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">noise identification</a> of the run, unless the option <b>Noise type</b> states it. It sets the equivalent degrees of freedom, and with them the confidence interval, and it selects the bias factor of the total and Thêo estimators.</p>",
    ] },
    { id: "Confidence-intervals", level: 2, title: "Confidence intervals", html: [
      "<h2>Confidence intervals</h2>",
      "<p>The interval of a deviation is that of a <span class=\"math\">χ<sup>2</sup></span> variable with the equivalent degrees of freedom (EDF) of the estimate, at the level of the option <b>Confidence intervals</b>. The EDF depends on the estimator, the noise type, the averaging time and the length of the record, and it falls toward long averaging times, where the intervals widen. The level of 68.3 % is that of one standard deviation of a normal variable. <a href=\"https://ianlap.github.io/SigmaTau.jl/dev/explanation/confidence/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Confidence intervals</a> gives the formulas and says how well the intervals hold.</p>",
      "<p>MTIE and TIE rms have no interval.</p>",
    ] },
    { id: "Bias-correction", level: 2, title: "Bias correction", html: [
      "<h2>Bias correction</h2>",
      "<p>The total estimators and Thêo1 reuse the record to gain degrees of freedom, and each then differs from the deviation it stands for by a factor that depends on the noise type. The option <b>Bias correction</b> chooses the factors:</p>",
      "<table><thead><tr><th>Choice</th><th>Factors</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><b>Exact</b></td><td>Those of this package, for every noise type and averaging factor</td></tr><tr><td><b>Published tables</b></td><td>Those of SP1065 and of the papers that define the estimators</td></tr><tr><td><b>As Stable32</b></td><td>Those that Stable32 applies to each estimator</td></tr><tr><td><b>None</b></td><td>No correction, as allantools returns</td></tr></tbody></table>",
      "<p><a href=\"https://ianlap.github.io/SigmaTau.jl/dev/explanation/bias/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Bias correction</a> lists the factors and their sources, and shows what each choice does to each estimator.</p>",
    ] },
  ] },
];
